begin;
update public.course_chapters set lesson_html = $less$<div class="pA"><div class="opener"><div class="k">Part A - Arithmetic · Chapter 1</div><h1>Number Basics</h1><div class="sub">BODMAS, decimals, fractions and mixed fractions</div></div>
<p>Every question in Paper 1 maths, even a long data question, finally comes down to simple steps with whole numbers, fractions and decimals. If these steps are quick and sure, every later chapter feels easier. A real PYQ from January 2026 was built only on the ideas in this chapter, and you will solve it at the end.</p>
<div class="chal"><div class="chh">⏱ 10-second challenge</div><div>Imran in Srinagar and Teresa in Kochi both work out 48 ÷ 4 × 2 without a calculator. Imran gets 6 and Teresa gets 24. Who is right?</div><div class="chw">Make a quick guess. The answer is in section 1.1.</div></div>
<div class="goals"><h4>By the end of this chapter you will be able to</h4><ul><li>solve any chain of +, −, × and ÷ in the right order (BODMAS);</li><li>move easily between decimals and fractions;</li><li>divide by a decimal without confusion;</li><li>find a fraction of a number, and solve "part left over" word problems;</li><li>add and subtract mixed fractions quickly.</li></ul></div>


<h2><span class="n">1.1</span>BODMAS: which step comes first?</h2>
<p>Farhan and Meenakshi solve 18 + 6 × 4 on paper. Farhan says 96, Meenakshi says 42. Both feel sure. Who is right?</p>
<p>Farhan went from left to right: 18 + 6 = 24, then 24 × 4 = 96. Meenakshi did the multiplication first: 6 × 4 = 24, then 18 + 24 = 42. Maths needs one fixed rule, otherwise the same question gives two answers. That rule is <b>BODMAS</b>, and Meenakshi followed it. The answer is 42.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 196' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='30' y='8' width='230' height='34' rx='6' fill='#17423B'/><text x='48' y='30' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>1</text><text x='66' y='30' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Brackets  ( )</text><text x='268' y='30' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>open them first</text><rect x='70' y='50' width='230' height='34' rx='6' fill='#17423B'/><text x='88' y='72' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>2</text><text x='106' y='72' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Of: powers, roots, "of"</text><text x='308' y='72' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>e.g. 3/4 of 160</text><rect x='110' y='92' width='230' height='34' rx='6' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='128' y='114' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3</text><text x='146' y='114' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>÷ and ×  (same step)</text><text x='348' y='114' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>go left to right</text><rect x='150' y='134' width='230' height='34' rx='6' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='168' y='156' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>4</text><text x='186' y='156' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>+ and −  (same step)</text><text x='388' y='156' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>go left to right</text><line x1='12' y1='14' x2='12' y2='172' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='12' y='188' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>do first → do last</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Brackets → Of → Division and Multiplication → Addition and Subtraction<small>D and M are on the same step, so do whichever comes first from the left. Same for A and S.</small></div>
<div class='think'><b>💡 Think of it like this:</b> BODMAS is like a queue at a railway counter. Brackets stand first in the queue. Division and multiplication stand together in the next place, so whoever is ahead (on the left) goes first.</div>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> Simplify 25 + 5 × 4 − 18 ÷ 3.<p>First the × and ÷: 5 × 4 = 20 and 18 ÷ 3 = 6. Then left to right: 25 + 20 − 6 = <b>39</b>.</p></div>
<div class="rev"><b>⏱ Challenge answer:</b> Teresa is right, the answer is 24. Imran did 4 × 2 first because "M" looks earlier in BODMAS, but D and M are on the same step. Go left to right: 48 ÷ 4 = 12, then 12 × 2 = 24. Doing 4 × 2 first gives 48 ÷ 8 = 6, and that wrong answer is often one of the options.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>20 − 12 ÷ 4 × 2 = ?</li><li>(6 + 4) × 3 − 5 = ?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 12 ÷ 4 = 3, 3 × 2 = 6, 20 − 6 = 14 · 2. 10 × 3 − 5 = 25</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">1.2</span>Decimals and fractions are two names for the same thing</h2>
<p>When you buy milk, half a litre and 0.5 litre are the same packet. Decimals and fractions are just two ways of writing the same amount. The easiest way to feel this is with money.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 116' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='40' y='18' width='95' height='40' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='87' y='43' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>25 paise</text><rect x='135' y='18' width='95' height='40' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='182' y='43' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>25 paise</text><rect x='230' y='18' width='95' height='40' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='277' y='43' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>25 paise</text><rect x='325' y='18' width='95' height='40' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='372' y='43' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>25 paise</text><text x='230' y='12' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>₹1 = 100 paise</text><line x1='135' y1='58' x2='135' y2='70' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='135' y='84' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>0.25 = 1/4</text><line x1='230' y1='58' x2='230' y2='70' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='230' y='84' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>0.5 = 1/2</text><line x1='325' y1='58' x2='325' y2='70' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='325' y='84' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>0.75 = 3/4</text><line x1='420' y1='58' x2='420' y2='70' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='420' y='84' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>1</text><text x='230' y='108' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>A decimal is just "out of 100": 0.25 means 25 out of 100, that is 1/4.</text></svg></div>
<p>Learn these eight pairs by heart. They come again and again in percentages, profit and data questions, and they save a lot of time.</p>
<table class="data"><tr><th>Decimal</th><th>Fraction</th><th>Decimal</th><th>Fraction</th></tr>
<tr><td>0.5</td><td><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span></td><td>0.125</td><td><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>8</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>0.25</td><td><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span></td><td>0.2</td><td><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>5</span></span></td></tr>
<tr><td>0.75</td><td><span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span></td><td>0.04</td><td><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>25</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>0.1</td><td><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>10</span></span></td><td>0.05</td><td><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>20</span></span></td></tr></table>
<p><b>Multiplying decimals:</b> forget the points, multiply the plain numbers, then put back as many decimal places as there were in total. For 1.5 × 0.4: 15 × 4 = 60. There were 2 decimal places in all, so the answer is 0.60 = 0.6.</p>
<div class='think'><b>💡 Think of it like this:</b> when a question mixes decimals and fractions, like 0.25 + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> + 0.75, first write everything in one form. Two languages in one sentence confuse anyone.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Write 0.75 as a fraction, and <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>8</span></span> as a decimal.</li><li>0.2 + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>5</span></span> = ?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span> and 0.125 · 2. 0.2 + 0.2 = 0.4</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">1.3</span>Dividing by a decimal</h2>
<p>How many 5-paise coins make ₹2? Since ₹2 is 200 paise, the answer is 200 ÷ 5 = 40. In rupees, that same question is 2 ÷ 0.05. So 2 ÷ 0.05 = 40.</p>
<p>Notice what happened: we changed rupees into paise, which multiplied <b>both</b> numbers by 100. That is the whole trick.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 120' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='8' y='26' width='128' height='58' rx='7' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='72.0' y='46' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>the question</text><text x='72.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>2 ÷ 0.05</text><rect x='166' y='26' width='128' height='58' rx='7' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='230.0' y='46' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>shift 2 places in both</text><text x='230.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>200 ÷ 5</text><rect x='324' y='26' width='128' height='58' rx='7' fill='#17423B'/><text x='388.0' y='46' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>answer</text><text x='388.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>40</text><line x1='138' y1='55' x2='162' y2='55' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><line x1='296' y1='55' x2='320' y2='55' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='230' y='112' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Same shift on top and bottom, so the answer does not change.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Move the decimal point the same number of places in both numbers<small>until the number you divide by becomes a whole number</small></div>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#17423B;color:#fff">T</div><div class="bub"><b>Tenzin:</b> How can 2 ÷ 0.05 be 40? I thought dividing always makes a number smaller.</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> Only when you divide by a number bigger than 1. Dividing by something smaller than 1 gives a bigger answer, just as many 5-paise coins fit in two rupees.</div></div></div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>3 ÷ 0.5 = ?</li><li>2.4 ÷ 0.08 = ?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 30 ÷ 5 = 6 · 2. 240 ÷ 8 = 30</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">1.4</span>A fraction of a number</h2>
<p>"<span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span> of 160" sounds like a formula, but it is a simple sharing idea. Cut 160 into 4 equal parts and take 3 of them.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 108' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><text x='230' y='14' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>160</text><path d='M40,22 L40,28 L420,28 L420,22' fill='none' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='40' y='34' width='95' height='40' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='87' y='59' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>40</text><rect x='135' y='34' width='95' height='40' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='182' y='59' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>40</text><rect x='230' y='34' width='95' height='40' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='277' y='59' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>40</text><rect x='325' y='34' width='95' height='40' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='372' y='59' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>40</text><text x='230' y='98' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Cut 160 into 4 equal parts: each part is 40. Take 3 parts: 3/4 of 160 = 120.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">First find one part, then take as many parts as you need<small><span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span> of 160: 160 ÷ 4 = 40, then 3 × 40 = 120</small></div>
<p><b>"What is left?" questions.</b> Exams love this type. Suppose Lalremruati, a nurse in Shillong, spends <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> of her salary on rent and <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> on food, and ₹5,000 is left. What is her salary? Draw the salary as a bar cut into 6 parts (6 is the smallest number that both 2 and 3 divide).</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 120' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='40' y='30' width='63' height='40' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='103' y='30' width='63' height='40' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='166' y='30' width='63' height='40' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='229' y='30' width='63' height='40' rx='0' fill='#FBE7C6' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='292' y='30' width='63' height='40' rx='0' fill='#FBE7C6' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='355' y='30' width='63' height='40' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='134' y='22' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>rent: 1/2 = 3 parts</text><text x='292' y='22' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>food: 1/3 = 2 parts</text><text x='386' y='55' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>₹5,000</text><text x='386' y='88' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>left: 1 part</text><text x='230' y='112' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>1 part = ₹5,000, so the full salary (6 parts) = ₹30,000.</text></svg></div>
<div class='think'><b>💡 Think of it like this:</b> always find the fraction that is <b>left</b> and match it with the money that is left. Here 1 − <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> − <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>6</span></span> is left, and that <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>6</span></span> is ₹5,000.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li><span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span> of 90 = ?</li><li>Joseph spends <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> of his salary on rent and <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>5</span></span> on food. ₹6,000 is left. Find his salary.</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 90 ÷ 3 = 30, 2 × 30 = 60 · 2. Left = 1 − <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> − <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>5</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>10</span></span> → <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>10</span></span> = 6,000 → salary ₹20,000</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">1.5</span>Mixed fractions</h2>
<p>4⅔ simply means 4 full and ⅔ more, like 4 full rotis and two-thirds of another. When you add or subtract mixed fractions, handle the full ones and the parts separately.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> 3¼ + 2¾.<p>Full ones: 3 + 2 = 5. Parts: ¼ + ¾ = 1. Total: 5 + 1 = <b>6</b>.</p></div>
<div class='think'><b>💡 Think of it like this:</b> look for parts that cancel before you calculate. In 4⅔ + 3½ − 1⅔, the ⅔ and the −⅔ cancel at once, so it is just 4 + 3½ − 1 = 6½. This is exactly the step from the January 2026 PYQ.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>5½ + 2½ = ?</li><li>3¾ − 1¼ = ?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 7 + 1 = 8 · 2. 2 + ½ = 2½</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">1.6</span>Your number toolkit</h2>
<p>Speed in Paper 1 does not come from tricks. It comes from knowing basic facts without thinking. Make these three your homework for the whole book: <b>tables up to 20</b>, <b>squares up to 30</b> and <b>cubes up to 15</b>. Here are the squares and cubes in one place. Read them once a day for two weeks.</p>
<table class="facts"><tr><td>1² = 1</td><td>11² = 121</td><td>21² = 441</td><td>1³ = 1</td><td>11³ = 1331</td></tr><tr><td>2² = 4</td><td>12² = 144</td><td>22² = 484</td><td>2³ = 8</td><td>12³ = 1728</td></tr><tr><td>3² = 9</td><td>13² = 169</td><td>23² = 529</td><td>3³ = 27</td><td>13³ = 2197</td></tr><tr><td>4² = 16</td><td>14² = 196</td><td>24² = 576</td><td>4³ = 64</td><td>14³ = 2744</td></tr><tr><td>5² = 25</td><td>15² = 225</td><td>25² = 625</td><td>5³ = 125</td><td>15³ = 3375</td></tr><tr><td>6² = 36</td><td>16² = 256</td><td>26² = 676</td><td>6³ = 216</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>7² = 49</td><td>17² = 289</td><td>27² = 729</td><td>7³ = 343</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>8² = 64</td><td>18² = 324</td><td>28² = 784</td><td>8³ = 512</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>9² = 81</td><td>19² = 361</td><td>29² = 841</td><td>9³ = 729</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>10² = 100</td><td>20² = 400</td><td>30² = 900</td><td>10³ = 1000</td><td></td></tr></table>

<h2><span class="n">1.7</span>How to solve any simplification question</h2>
<div class="steps3"><div><b>1. Mark the order</b>Circle the brackets, then the × and ÷. Do them before + and −.</div><div><b>2. One form only</b>Change everything into decimals or everything into fractions.</div><div><b>3. Check with options</b>Estimate roughly. The wrong options are often the "left to right" mistakes.</div></div>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 300' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='65' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='395' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='48' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230' y='147.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>NUMBER</text><text x='230' y='164.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>BASICS</text><rect x='8' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>BODMAS</text><text x='80' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>B → O → DM → AS</text><rect x='308' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>D and M together</text><text x='380' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>go left to right</text><rect x='-7' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='65' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Decimal = fraction</text><text x='65' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>0.25 = 1/4</text><rect x='323' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='395' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>÷ by a decimal</text><text x='395' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>shift both points</text><rect x='8' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Fraction of</text><text x='80' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>one part first</text><rect x='308' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Mixed fractions</text><text x='380' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>wholes and parts apart</text></svg></div><p class="cap">The whole chapter on one page. Revise from here before the exam.</p>
<div class="card"><h4>Quick card</h4><table><tr><td>Order of steps</td><td>Brackets → Of → ÷ × (left to right) → + − (left to right)</td></tr><tr><td>Decimal × decimal</td><td>multiply plain numbers, then count decimal places</td></tr><tr><td>÷ by a decimal</td><td>shift the point equally in both numbers</td></tr><tr><td><span class="fr"><span>a</span><span>b</span></span> of N</td><td>(N ÷ b) × a</td></tr><tr><td>Part left</td><td>1 − (fractions spent)</td></tr><tr><td>Mixed fractions</td><td>add wholes and parts separately</td></tr></table></div></div>$less$ where no = 1;
update public.course_chapters set lesson_html = $less$<div class="pA"><div class="opener"><div class="k">Part A - Arithmetic · Chapter 2</div><h1>Fractions</h1><div class="sub">Comparing, ordering, adding and the three ways to see which is bigger</div></div>
<p>Half a litre of milk, a quarter kilo of tomatoes: a fraction is one whole cut into equal parts, of which we take some. In Paper 1 the usual fraction question gives four or five fractions to put in order, so this chapter gives you three quick ways to compare them. You will meet three real past questions (PYQs) here.</p>
<div class="chal"><div class="chh">⏱ 10-second challenge</div><div>In a quiz, Mary from Kohima has answered 13 of 25 questions and Bikash from Patna has answered 3 of 5. Who has finished the bigger fraction of the quiz?</div><div class="chw">Make a quick guess. The answer is in section 2.3.</div></div>
<div class="goals"><h4>By the end of this chapter you will be able to</h4><ul><li>compare two fractions at a glance when the tops or bottoms match;</li><li>add and subtract fractions by making the pieces the same size;</li><li>order four or five fractions using decimals;</li><li>compare two fractions by cross-multiplying;</li><li>compare fractions close to 1 using the "gap from 1" idea.</li></ul></div>


<h2><span class="n">2.1</span>What the top and the bottom tell you</h2>
<p>Every fraction has two numbers. The bottom number (denominator) tells you into how many equal pieces the whole is cut. The top number (numerator) tells you how many of those pieces you take. In 3/8, a roti is cut into 8 equal pieces and you take 3.</p>
<p>Now two easy cases. If two fractions have the <b>same bottom</b>, the pieces are the same size, so more pieces means more roti: <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>9</span></span> is bigger than <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>9</span></span>. If they have the <b>same top</b>, you take the same number of pieces, so the bigger pieces win. Cutting into 7 gives bigger pieces than cutting into 8.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 134' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='70.0' y='14' width='51.4' height='34' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='121.4' y='14' width='51.4' height='34' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='172.9' y='14' width='51.4' height='34' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='224.3' y='14' width='51.4' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='275.7' y='14' width='51.4' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='327.1' y='14' width='51.4' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='378.6' y='14' width='51.4' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='38' y='37' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='16' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3/7</text><text x='147.14285714285714' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3 pieces</text><rect x='70.0' y='64' width='45.0' height='34' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='115.0' y='64' width='45.0' height='34' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='160.0' y='64' width='45.0' height='34' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='205.0' y='64' width='45.0' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='250.0' y='64' width='45.0' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='295.0' y='64' width='45.0' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='340.0' y='64' width='45.0' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='385.0' y='64' width='45.0' height='34' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='38' y='87' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='16' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3/8</text><text x='137.5' y='86' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3 pieces</text><line x1='224.3' y1='8' x2='224.3' y2='104' stroke='#D95D39' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/><text x='230' y='126' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Same number of pieces, but sevenths are bigger pieces than eighths.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Same bottom: bigger top is bigger. Same top: smaller bottom is bigger.<small><span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>9</span></span> > <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>9</span></span> and <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>7</span></span> > <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>8</span></span></small></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> in the "same top" case, many students pick the fraction with the bigger bottom because 8 looks bigger than 7. Remember the roti: cut it into more pieces and each piece gets smaller.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Which is bigger: <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>11</span></span> or <span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>11</span></span>?</li><li>Which is bigger: <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>9</span></span> or <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>7</span></span>?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. same bottom, so <span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>11</span></span> · 2. same top, so <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>7</span></span> (sevenths are bigger pieces)</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">2.2</span>Adding and subtracting: make the pieces the same size</h2>
<p>Suppose Zoya gives you <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> of a cake and Arjun gives you <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> of the same cake. How much do you have? You cannot just add the pieces, because a third and a quarter are different sizes. First cut both into pieces of the same size. Both 3 and 4 divide 12, so think in twelfths: <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>12</span></span> and <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>12</span></span>.</p>
<div class="rule">Change the fractions to the same bottom, then add or subtract the tops<small><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>12</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>12</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>12</span></span></small></div>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span> − <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>6</span></span>.<p>Both 4 and 6 divide 12. So <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>9</span><span>12</span></span> and <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>6</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>12</span></span>. Then <span class="fr"><span>9</span><span>12</span></span> − <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>12</span></span> = <b><span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>12</span></span></b>.</p></div>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#F2A541;color:#17423B">A</div><div class="bub"><b>Ayesha:</b> Why not just add tops and bottoms? <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>7</span></span> is much quicker.</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> Quicker, but impossible. <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>7</span></span> is smaller than 1/3, yet you added more cake. If an option looks like 2/7, it is the trap.</div></div></div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> = ?</li><li><span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span> − <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> = ?</li><li><span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>12</span></span> = ?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>4</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span> · 2. <span class="fr"><span>8</span><span>12</span></span> − <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>12</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>12</span></span> · 3. <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>12</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>12</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>12</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>8</span><span>12</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span></div></div>

<h2><span class="n">2.3</span>Method 1: change to decimals</h2>
<p>Four colleagues in a Pune office open their tiffin boxes. Harpreet has eaten <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>8</span></span> of the food, Thomas 5/12, Kavya <span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>10</span></span> and Abdul <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>5</span></span>. Who has eaten the most? The bottoms are all different, so neither rule from section 2.1 helps. The easiest way is to change every fraction into a decimal. Then it is like comparing prices in rupees.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 142' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='30' y1='60' x2='430' y2='60' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2'/><line x1='30.0' y1='55' x2='30.0' y2='65' stroke='#9fb3ae' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='96.7' y1='55' x2='96.7' y2='65' stroke='#9fb3ae' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='163.3' y1='55' x2='163.3' y2='65' stroke='#9fb3ae' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='230.0' y1='55' x2='230.0' y2='65' stroke='#9fb3ae' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='296.7' y1='55' x2='296.7' y2='65' stroke='#9fb3ae' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='363.3' y1='55' x2='363.3' y2='65' stroke='#9fb3ae' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='430.0' y1='55' x2='430.0' y2='65' stroke='#9fb3ae' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='30.0' y='112' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#9fb3ae' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>0.3</text><text x='430.0' y='112' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#9fb3ae' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>0.9</text><circle cx='80.0' cy='60' r='6' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80.0' y='26' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3/8</text><text x='80.0' y='42' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>0.375</text><circle cx='107.8' cy='60' r='6' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='107.77777777777779' y='86' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>5/12</text><text x='107.77777777777779' y='101' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>≈ 0.42</text><circle cx='296.7' cy='60' r='6' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='296.66666666666663' y='26' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>7/10</text><text x='296.66666666666663' y='42' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>0.70</text><circle cx='363.3' cy='60' r='6' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='363.3333333333333' y='86' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>4/5</text><text x='363.3333333333333' y='101' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>0.80</text><text x='230' y='134' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Change each fraction to a decimal, then read them from left to right.</text></svg></div>
<p><b>How to get the decimal quickly.</b> Divide the top by the bottom, and stop after two decimal places. For <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>12</span></span>: 12 does not go into 5, so write 0. and put a zero: 50 ÷ 12 = 4, remainder 2. Put another zero: 20 ÷ 12 = 1, remainder 8. So <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>12</span></span> = 0.41 and a little more, about 0.42. That is enough. Many fractions you already know from Chapter 1: <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> = 0.5, <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> = 0.25, <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span> = 0.75, <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>8</span></span> = 0.125.</p>
<div class="rule">Divide top by bottom, keep two decimal places, then compare<small>best when there are 3, 4 or 5 fractions</small></div>
<div class="rev"><b>⏱ Challenge answer:</b> Bikash. Big numbers do not make a big fraction: Mary has <span class="fr"><span>13</span><span>25</span></span> = 0.52, Bikash has <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>5</span></span> = 0.6. Always look at the value, not at the size of the numbers.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Write <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>6</span></span> as a decimal with two places.</li><li>Arrange in ascending order: 3/4, 4/7, <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>5</span></span>.</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 0.83 · 2. 0.75, 0.57, 0.4, so <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>5</span></span> &lt; <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>7</span></span> &lt; <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span></div></div>

<h2><span class="n">2.4</span>Method 2: cross-multiplication</h2>
<p>When there are only two fractions, there is an even faster way that needs no division at all. Why does it work? To compare <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>5</span></span> and <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>8</span></span> fairly, we could put both over the same bottom, 5 × 8 = 40. Then <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>5</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>24</span><span>40</span></span> and <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>8</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>25</span><span>40</span></span>. Now the bottoms match, so we only compare the tops: 24 and 25. Notice that 24 is just 3 × 8 and 25 is just 5 × 5. Cross-multiplication is a short way of writing exactly this.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 174' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><text x='150' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='24' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3</text><line x1='134' y1='46' x2='166' y2='46' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2'/><text x='150' y='74' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='24' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>5</text><text x='310' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='24' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>5</text><line x1='294' y1='46' x2='326' y2='46' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2'/><text x='310' y='74' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='24' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>8</text><line x1='166' y1='30' x2='292' y2='64' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><line x1='294' y1='30' x2='168' y2='64' stroke='#D95D39' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ar)'/><rect x='80' y='92' width='140' height='32' rx='6' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='150' y='113' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3 × 8 = 24</text><rect x='240' y='92' width='140' height='32' rx='6' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='310' y='113' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>5 × 5 = 25</text><text x='230' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Each product stays on the side of its top number. 25 is bigger,</text><text x='230' y='165' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>so the right side wins: 5/8 is bigger than 3/5.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">To compare <span class="fr"><span>a</span><span>b</span></span> and c/d, compare a × d with c × b<small>the bigger product is on the side of the bigger fraction</small></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> keep each product on the side of its <b>top</b> number. 3 × 8 belongs to 3/5, even though the 8 comes from the other fraction. If you mix up the sides, you will pick exactly the wrong answer.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Which is bigger: <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>7</span></span> or <span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>10</span></span>?</li><li>Which is bigger: <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>8</span></span> or <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>5</span></span>?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 5 × 10 = 50, 7 × 7 = 49, so <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>7</span></span> · 2. 3 × 5 = 15, 2 × 8 = 16, so <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>5</span></span></div></div>

<h2><span class="n">2.5</span>Method 3: the gap from 1</h2>
<p>Some exam questions give fractions like 6/7, <span class="fr"><span>11</span><span>13</span></span> and <span class="fr"><span>16</span><span>19</span></span>. All of them are a little less than 1, and their decimals are close together, so dividing is slow. Here is a better idea. Think of a water tank that is almost full. To know which tank has more water, look at how much is <b>missing</b>. The tank with the smaller empty part has more water.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 166' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='70' y='10' width='240.0' height='28' rx='0' fill='#17423B'/><rect x='310.0' y='10' width='60.0' height='28' rx='0' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='40' y='30' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>4/5</text><text x='380' y='29' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>gap 1/5</text><rect x='70' y='50' width='257.1' height='28' rx='0' fill='#17423B'/><rect x='327.1' y='50' width='42.9' height='28' rx='0' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='40' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>6/7</text><text x='380' y='69' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>gap 1/7</text><rect x='70' y='90' width='270.0' height='28' rx='0' fill='#17423B'/><rect x='340.0' y='90' width='30.0' height='28' rx='0' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='40' y='110' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>9/10</text><text x='380' y='109' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>gap 1/10</text><line x1='370' y1='4' x2='370' y2='124' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='370' y='136' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>1 whole</text><text x='230' y='158' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Smallest gap, biggest fraction: 9/10 > 6/7 > 4/5</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Find the gap from 1 for each fraction. The smaller the gap, the bigger the fraction.<small><span class="fr"><span>6</span><span>7</span></span> has gap 1/7, and <span class="fr"><span>11</span><span>13</span></span> has gap <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>13</span></span></small></div>
<p>Sometimes the gaps themselves are hard to compare, like <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>7</span></span> and <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>13</span></span>. Make the tops equal: <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>13</span></span> is the same as <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>6.5</span></span>. One piece out of 6.5 is bigger than one piece out of 7, so <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>13</span></span> is the bigger gap. The smaller gap is 1/7, so <b><span class="fr"><span>6</span><span>7</span></span> is bigger than <span class="fr"><span>11</span><span>13</span></span></b>.</p>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> this method turns things upside down: the <b>bigger</b> gap means the <b>smaller</b> fraction. Write "small gap = big fraction" at the top of your rough sheet before you start.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Which is bigger: <span class="fr"><span>8</span><span>9</span></span> or <span class="fr"><span>9</span><span>10</span></span>?</li><li>Which is bigger: <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>7</span></span> or <span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>9</span></span>?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. gaps <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>9</span></span> and 1/10, smaller gap 1/10, so <span class="fr"><span>9</span><span>10</span></span> · 2. gaps <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>7</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3.5</span></span> and <span class="fr"><span>2</span><span>9</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span>.5, smaller gap 2/9, so <span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>9</span></span></div></div>

<h2><span class="n">2.6</span>Ordering many fractions: let the options help</h2>
<p>In the exam you rarely need the full order. The four options already give you four possible orders. Find the <b>smallest</b> fraction first and cross out every option that does not start with it. Often only one or two options are left after this single step.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> Arrange in ascending order: A = 4/5, B = 2/3, C = 7/9, D = <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>5</span></span>.<br>(1) D &lt; B &lt; C &lt; A &nbsp; (2) B &lt; D &lt; C &lt; A &nbsp; (3) D &lt; C &lt; B &lt; A &nbsp; (4) D &lt; B &lt; A &lt; C<p>Decimals: A = 0.8, B ≈ 0.67, C ≈ 0.78, D = 0.6. The smallest is D, so option 2 goes. The next is B, so option 3 goes. Options 1 and 4 now differ only at the end: A (0.8) is bigger than C (0.78), so A comes last. Answer: <b>option 1</b>.</p></div>
<div class='think'><b>💡 Think of it like this:</b> choosing the method is half the work. Two fractions: cross-multiply. Three to five fractions: decimals. All of them close to 1: gaps. You may also mix them, for example decimals first and then cross-multiplication for the two that are too close to call.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Arrange in ascending order: A = 3/7, B = 1/2, C = 2/5, D = <span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>9</span></span>.<br>(1) C &lt; A &lt; B &lt; D &nbsp; (2) A &lt; C &lt; B &lt; D &nbsp; (3) C &lt; A &lt; D &lt; B &nbsp; (4) A &lt; B &lt; C &lt; D</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: C = 0.4 is smallest (options 2 and 4 go), A ≈ 0.43 next, then B = 0.5, then D ≈ 0.56, so option (1)</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">2.7</span>How to solve any fraction question</h2>
<div class="steps3"><div><b>1. Look at the fractions</b>Same top or bottom? Two fractions only? All close to 1? This picks your method.</div><div><b>2. Find the ends</b>Find the smallest (or largest) first and cross out options.</div><div><b>3. Settle the rest</b>Compare only the fractions the remaining options disagree about.</div></div>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 300' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='65' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='395' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='48' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230' y='156.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>FRACTIONS</text><rect x='8' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Same bottom</text><text x='80' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>bigger top wins</text><rect x='308' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Same top</text><text x='380' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>smaller bottom wins</text><rect x='-7' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='65' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Add or subtract</text><text x='65' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>make bottoms equal</text><rect x='323' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='395' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Decimals</text><text x='395' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>for 3-5 fractions</text><rect x='8' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Cross-multiply</text><text x='80' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>for 2 fractions</text><rect x='308' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Gap from 1</text><text x='380' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>when all are near 1</text></svg></div><p class="cap">The whole chapter on one page. Revise from here before the exam.</p>
<div class="card"><h4>Quick card</h4><table><tr><td>Same bottom</td><td>bigger top is bigger (<span class="fr"><span>5</span><span>9</span></span> > <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>9</span></span>)</td></tr><tr><td>Same top</td><td>smaller bottom is bigger (<span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>7</span></span> > <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>8</span></span>)</td></tr><tr><td>Add or subtract</td><td>same bottom first: <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>4</span><span>12</span></span> + <span class="fr"><span>3</span><span>12</span></span> = <span class="fr"><span>7</span><span>12</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Decimals</td><td>top ÷ bottom, two places; best for 3-5 fractions</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-multiply</td><td><span class="fr"><span>a</span><span>b</span></span> vs <span class="fr"><span>c</span><span>d</span></span>: compare a × d with c × b</td></tr><tr><td>Gap from 1</td><td>smaller gap = bigger fraction</td></tr><tr><td>Ordering MCQ</td><td>find the smallest first, cross out options</td></tr></table></div></div>$less$ where no = 2;
update public.course_chapters set lesson_html = $less$<div class="pA"><div class="opener"><div class="k">Part A - Arithmetic · Chapter 3</div><h1>Percentage Basics</h1><div class="sub">Per cent, the fraction table and finding parts and wholes</div></div>
<p>"50% off" in a Diwali sale, "8% interest" on a bank board, "72%" on a marksheet: percentages put everything on one scale, out of 100. Percentage change, profit, discount, interest and data tables all stand on this chapter, and you will meet two real past questions (PYQs) here.</p>
<div class="chal"><div class="chh">⏱ 10-second challenge</div><div>Gurpreet runs a chemist shop in Ludhiana. She gives 12% off on a bill of ₹25. How much money is that? Try to find 12% of 25 in your head.</div><div class="chw">Make a quick guess. The answer is in section 3.4.</div></div>
<div class="goals"><h4>By the end of this chapter you will be able to</h4><ul><li>explain what "per cent" means and write a percentage as a fraction;</li><li>change common fractions into percentages from memory;</li><li>find any percentage of a number using 10% and 1%;</li><li>find what percentage one number is of another;</li><li>find the whole when a part and its percentage are given.</li></ul></div>


<h2><span class="n">3.1</span>Per cent means "out of 100"</h2>
<p>"Cent" means hundred, as in century (100 runs in cricket). So 25 per cent simply means 25 out of every 100. Picture a sheet of 100 small squares and colour 25 of them.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 134' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='40' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='52' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='64' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='76' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='88' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='100' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='112' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='124' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='136' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='148' y='8' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='40' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='52' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='64' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='76' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='88' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='100' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='112' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='124' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='136' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='148' y='20' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='40' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='52' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='64' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='76' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='88' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='100' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='112' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='124' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='136' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='148' y='32' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='40' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='52' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='64' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='76' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='88' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='100' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='112' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='124' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='136' y='44' width='12' height='12' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='148' y='44' width='12' height