<p>You do not need to count from A every time. Learn five anchor letters, <b>EJOTY</b>: E = 5, J = 10, O = 15, T = 20, Y = 25. Then any letter is only one or two steps away from an anchor. For example, R is two before T, so R = 20 − 2 = 18. L is two after J, so L = 12.</p>
<div class="rule">Change every letter into its number before you look for a pattern<small>Anchors: E = 5, J = 10, O = 15, T = 20, Y = 25</small></div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>What is the number of Q? Use an anchor.</li><li>Which letter is number 22?</li><li>Which letter is number 13?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Q is 3 before T: 20 − 3 = 17 · 2. Y = 25, so 22 is 3 before Y = V · 3. O = 15, so 13 is 2 before O = M</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">15.2</span>Opposite letters and counting from the right</h2>
<p>Write A to M on one line and Z back to N under it. Each letter now sits over its "opposite". A sits over Z, B over Y, and so on. Look at what happens to their numbers.</p>
