<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#17423B;color:#fff">T</div><div class="bub"><b>Tenzin:</b> Why 27? There are only 26 letters.</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> Move one step right: the top number goes up by 1 and the bottom one goes down by 1. The total never changes, and it starts at A + Z = 1 + 26 = 27.</div></div></div>
<p>The same idea answers "which letter is n-th from the right end?". The 1st from the right is Z = 26, the 2nd is Y = 25, the 3rd is X = 24. Each time, the position from the right and the number add up to 27.</p>
<div class="rule">Opposite of a letter = 27 − its number.   n-th letter from the right = letter number 27 − n</div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> many students write 26 − n for "n-th from the right". Test it with n = 1: the 1st letter from the right is Z, which is 26 = 27 − 1, not 26 − 1 = 25. Always use 27.</div>
<div class="rev"><b>⏱ Challenge answer:</b> Q. The 10th letter from the right is letter number 27 − 10 = 17, which is Q. Using 26 − 10 = 16 gives P, one step off.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>What is the opposite letter of H?</li><li>Which letter is 5th from the right end of the alphabet?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 27 − 8 = 19 = S · 2. 27 − 5 = 22 = V</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">15.3</span>A simple letter series</h2>
<p>Suppose the token machine at a bank in Bhubaneswar calls C, G, K, O. Which token comes next? Do not stare at the letters. Write the numbers under them.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 138' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='22' y='40' width='54' height='44' rx='8' fill='#17423B'/><text x='49' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='18' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>C</text><text x='49' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3</text><path d='M62,38 Q97,6 130,36' fill='none' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='2.2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='97' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+4</text><rect x='118' y='40' width='54' height='44' rx='8' fill='#17423B'/><text x='145' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='18' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>G</text><text x='145' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>7</text><path d='M158,38 Q193,6 226,36' fill='none' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='2.2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='193' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+4</text><rect x='214' y='40' width='54' height='44' rx='8' fill='#17423B'/><text x='241' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='18' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>K</text><text x='241' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>11</text><path d='M254,38 Q289,6 322,36' fill='none' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='2.2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='289' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+4</text><rect x='310' y='40' width='54' height='44' rx='8' fill='#17423B'/><text x='337' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='18' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>O</text><text x='337' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>15</text><path d='M350,38 Q385,6 418,36' fill='none' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='2.2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='385' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+4</text><rect x='406' y='40' width='54' height='44' rx='8' fill='#D95D39'/><text x='433' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='18' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>?</text><text x='433' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>19 = S</text><text x='230' y='130' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>Write the numbers, find the jump, then turn the number back into a letter.</text></svg></div>
<p>Now it is a number series: 3, 7, 11, 15. The jump is +4 each time, so the next number is 19, which is S. Everything you learnt in Chapter 14 works here: equal jumps, growing jumps (+1, +2, +3 ...), falling jumps, and two series mixed together.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> Y, V, S, P, ?<p>Numbers: 25, 22, 19, 16. Each step is −3. Next: 16 − 3 = 13 = <b>M</b>.</p></div>
<p><b>Going past Z.</b> The alphabet is like a circular running track: after Z you come back to A. So if a number goes above 26, subtract 26. If it goes below 1, add 26.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> V, Y, B, E, ?<p>V = 22, Y = 25. The next step +3 gives 28, which is past Z. 28 − 26 = 2 = B, and B is exactly the next letter given. Then E = 5, and the next is 5 + 3 = 8 = <b>H</b>.</p></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> when you see Z, Y followed by A, B, do not decide the pattern is broken. Check the jump across the end: Y = 25 and B = 2 are +3 apart on the circular track (25 + 3 = 28, and 28 − 26 = 2).</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>C, D, F, I, ?</li><li>W, Z, C, F, ?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 3, 4, 6, 9: jumps +1, +2, +3, so next +4 = 13 = M · 2. 23, 26, 29 − 26 = 3, 6: jumps +3, so next 9 = I</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">15.4</span>Groups of letters: one position at a time</h2>
<p>Some questions give groups like DGK, FIM, HKO. Reading a whole group as one word does not help. Think of a train with three coaches. Each coach has its own journey. Follow the first coach of every group, then the second coach, then the third.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 154' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><text x='98' y='14' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>DGK</text><rect x='70' y='22' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='98' y='41' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>D (4)</text><line x1='128' y1='36' x2='166' y2='36' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='147' y='32' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><rect x='70' y='58' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='98' y='77' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>G (7)</text><line x1='128' y1='72' x2='166' y2='72' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='147' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><rect x='70' y='94' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='98' y='113' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>K (11)</text><line x1='128' y1='108' x2='166' y2='108' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='147' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><text x='198' y='14' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>FIM</text><rect x='170' y='22' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='198' y='41' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>F (6)</text><line x1='228' y1='36' x2='266' y2='36' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='247' y='32' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><rect x='170' y='58' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='198' y='77' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>I (9)</text><line x1='228' y1='72' x2='266' y2='72' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='247' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><rect x='170' y='94' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='198' y='113' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>M (13)</text><line x1='228' y1='108' x2='266' y2='108' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='247' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><text x='298' y='14' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>HKO</text><rect x='270' y='22' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='298' y='41' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>H (8)</text><line x1='328' y1='36' x2='366' y2='36' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='347' y='32' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><rect x='270' y='58' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='298' y='77' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>K (11)</text><line x1='328' y1='72' x2='366' y2='72' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='347' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><rect x='270' y='94' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='298' y='113' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>O (15)</text><line x1='328' y1='108' x2='366' y2='108' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='347' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+2</text><text x='398' y='14' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>? = JMQ</text><rect x='370' y='22' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#FBE7C6' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='398' y='41' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>J (10)</text><rect x='370' y='58' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#FBE7C6' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='398' y='77' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>M (13)</text><rect x='370' y='94' width='56' height='28' rx='5' fill='#FBE7C6' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='398' y='113' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Q (17)</text><text x='4' y='41' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>1st letters</text><text x='4' y='77' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>2nd letters</text><text x='4' y='113' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>3rd letters</text><text x='230' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Read across each row, not along each group.</text></svg></div>
<p>The 1st letters go D, F, H (4, 6, 8), so next is 10 = J. The 2nd letters go G, I, K (7, 9, 11), so next is 13 = M. The 3rd letters go K, M, O (11, 13, 15), so next is 17 = Q. The answer is <b>JMQ</b>.</p>
<div class="rule">Make columns: all 1st letters, all 2nd letters, all 3rd letters<small>Each column can have its own jump: one may go up while another goes down</small></div>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#F2A541;color:#17423B">A</div><div class="bub"><b>Ayesha:</b> Must I work out all three columns every time?</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> Rarely. Find the first letter of the answer and cross out options that do not start with it. Often only one or two are left.</div></div></div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>BDF, GIK, LNP, ?</li><li>CX, EV, GT, ?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 1st: B, G, L (+5) → Q; 2nd: D, I, N → S; 3rd: F, K, P → U; answer QSU · 2. 1st: C, E, G (+2) → I; 2nd: X, V, T (−2) → R; answer IR</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">15.5</span>Letters mixed with numbers</h2>
<p>Sometimes a term is a letter with a number, like B4 or C5F. Split each term into its parts and follow each part on its own, just as you did with groups. Often the number is linked to the letter's position.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> B4, D16, F36, ?<p>Letters: B, D, F (2, 4, 6), so next is H (8). Numbers: 4, 16, 36. These are 2 × 2, 4 × 4, 6 × 6, the square of the letter number. So the number with H is 8 × 8 = 64. The answer is <b>H64</b>.</p></div>
<div class='think'><b>💡 Think of it like this:</b> when a number sits next to a letter, first ask: is it the letter's own position, or that position squared, doubled or plus something? If not, treat the numbers as a separate series of their own, for example a list of prime numbers.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Z1, Y2, X3, ?</li><li>C6, E10, G14, ?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Letters go back one, numbers go up one: W4 · 2. Letters +2: I; number = 2 × letter number (3 × 2, 5 × 2, 7 × 2): 9 × 2 = 18, so I18</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">15.6</span>How to solve any letter series question</h2>
<div class="steps3"><div><b>1. Write numbers</b>Put the position number under every letter. Use EJOTY to be quick.</div><div><b>2. Split it up</b>Groups or mixed terms? Make one column for each position.</div><div><b>3. Find the jump</b>Use the Chapter 14 checklist. Past Z, subtract 26. Then check the options.</div></div>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 300' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='65' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='395' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='48' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230' y='147.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>LETTER</text><text x='230' y='164.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>SERIES</text><rect x='8' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>A = 1 ... Z = 26</text><text x='80' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>positions first</text><rect x='308' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>EJOTY</text><text x='380' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>5, 10, 15, 20, 25</text><rect x='-7' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='65' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Opposite letters</text><text x='65' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>pair adds to 27</text><rect x='323' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='395' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>From the right</text><text x='395' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>n-th = 27 − n</text><rect x='8' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Groups</text><text x='80' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>one position at a time</text><rect x='308' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Past Z</text><text x='380' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>subtract 26</text></svg></div><p class="cap">The whole chapter on one page. Revise from here before the exam.</p>
<div class="card"><h4>Quick card</h4><table><tr><td>Positions</td><td>A = 1, B = 2 ... Z = 26</td></tr><tr><td>Anchors</td><td>E 5, J 10, O 15, T 20, Y 25</td></tr><tr><td>Opposite letter</td><td>27 − number (A and Z, M and N)</td></tr><tr><td>n-th from right</td><td>letter number 27 − n</td></tr><tr><td>Past Z</td><td>subtract 26 (Z + 1 = A)</td></tr><tr><td>Groups</td><td>follow each position separately</td></tr></table></div></div>$less$ where no = 15;
update public.course_chapters set lesson_html = $less$<div class="pB"><div class="opener"><div class="k">Part B - Reasoning · Chapter 16</div><h1>Coding-Decoding</h1><div class="sub">Letter shifts, opposite letters, number codes and word codes</div></div>
<p>As children, many of us played a secret-message game: write every letter one step ahead, so that CAT becomes DBU. Coding-decoding is the same game. You find the rule from one example and use it on a new word. It needs the letter numbers, EJOTY and opposite letters from Chapter 15, and you will meet 7 real past questions here.</p>
<div class="chal"><div class="chh">⏱ 10-second challenge</div><div>Nandini in Jaipur sends a secret note to Joseph in Kochi. In their code, DOG is written as EQJ. How is CAT written?</div><div class="chw">Make a quick guess. The answer is in section 16.1.</div></div>
<div class="goals"><h4>By the end of this chapter you will be able to</h4><ul><li>find the coding rule from one example;</li><li>use letter shifts, reversed words and opposite letters;</li><li>decode number codes: sums, positions, +n, squares and reversed order;</li><li>crack codes where each letter has its own digit;</li><li>find the code of a word from a few coded sentences.</li></ul></div>

<h2><span class="n">16.1</span>Moving every letter forward or back</h2>
<p>The simplest code moves every letter by the same number of steps. To find the step, write the number of one letter in the word and the number of the matching letter in the code, and subtract.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 192' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='95' y='10' width='60' height='40' rx='7' fill='#17423B'/><text x='125' y='37' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>C</text><text x='125' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>3</text><line x1='125' y1='72' x2='125' y2='100' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='147' y='92' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+1</text><rect x='95' y='104' width='60' height='40' rx='7' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='125' y='131' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>D</text><text x='125' y='160' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>4</text><rect x='195' y='10' width='60' height='40' rx='7' fill='#17423B'/><text x='225' y='37' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>A</text><text x='225' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>1</text><line x1='225' y1='72' x2='225' y2='100' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='247' y='92' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+1</text><rect x='195' y='104' width='60' height='40' rx='7' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='225' y='131' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>B</text><text x='225' y='160' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>2</text><rect x='295' y='10' width='60' height='40' rx='7' fill='#17423B'/><text x='325' y='37' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>T</text><text x='325' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>20</text><line x1='325' y1='72' x2='325' y2='100' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='347' y='92' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+1</text><rect x='295' y='104' width='60' height='40' rx='7' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='325' y='131' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>U</text><text x='325' y='160' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>21</text><text x='40' y='37' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>word</text><text x='40' y='131' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>code</text><text x='230' y='184' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>Every letter moves one step forward. Apply the same step to the new word.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Code letter − word letter = the step. Use the same step for every letter of the new word<small>If a number goes past Z, subtract 26; if it goes below A, add 26</small></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> do not check only the first letter. Some codes use +1 for the first letter, +2 for the second, +3 for the third. Check at least two letters before you decide the step.</div>
<div class="rev"><b>⏱ Challenge answer:</b> DCW, not DBU. D → E is +1, O → Q is +2, G → J is +3. So C + 1 = D, A + 2 = C, T + 3 = W.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>If BOOK is coded as CPPL, how is DESK coded?</li><li>If each letter moves 2 steps back, how is FISH written?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Each letter +1: EFTL · 2. F6 → 4 = D, I9 → 7 = G, S19 → 17 = Q, H8 → 6 = F: DGQF</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">16.2</span>Reversed words and opposite letters</h2>
<p>Two other rules appear often. In the first, the word is simply written backwards, like reading a car number plate in a mirror. In the second, every letter is replaced by its opposite letter from Chapter 15, the one that makes a total of 27 with it.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 152' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='222' height='150' rx='8' fill='#EEF4F2'/><rect x='238' y='0' width='222' height='150' rx='8' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='111' y='22' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Reverse the word</text><text x='349' y='22' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Opposite letters</text><rect x='20' y='36' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#17423B'/><text x='39' y='58' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>F</text><rect x='68' y='36' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#17423B'/><text x='87' y='58' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>A</text><rect x='116' y='36' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#17423B'/><text x='135' y='58' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>C</text><rect x='164' y='36' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#17423B'/><text x='183' y='58' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>E</text><rect x='20' y='100' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='39' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>E</text><rect x='68' y='100' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='87' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>C</text><rect x='116' y='100' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='135' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>A</text><rect x='164' y='100' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='183' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>F</text><line x1='111' y1='72' x2='111' y2='95' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='160' y='89' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>read backwards</text><rect x='258' y='36' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#17423B'/><text x='277' y='58' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>F</text><text x='277' y='88' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>6+21</text><rect x='258' y='100' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='277' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>U</text><rect x='306' y='36' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#17423B'/><text x='325' y='58' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>A</text><text x='325' y='88' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>1+26</text><rect x='306' y='100' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='325' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Z</text><rect x='354' y='36' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#17423B'/><text x='373' y='58' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>C</text><text x='373' y='88' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>3+24</text><rect x='354' y='100' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='373' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>X</text><rect x='402' y='36' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#17423B'/><text x='421' y='58' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>E</text><text x='421' y='88' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>5+22</text><rect x='402' y='100' width='38' height='32' rx='5' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='421' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>V</text><text x='349' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>each pair adds up to 27</text></svg></div>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#17423B;color:#fff">T</div><div class="bub"><b>Tenzin:</b> How do I tell a reversed word from an opposite-letter code?</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> Same letters in a new order? Probably reversed. All new letters from the other end of the alphabet? Check opposite letters.</div></div></div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>If GO is coded as TL, how is AT coded?</li><li>If NOTE is written as ETON, how is PLANT written?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Opposite letters: A1 → 26 = Z, T20 → 7 = G: ZG · 2. Reversed: TNALP</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">16.3</span>Letters into numbers</h2>
<p>Most past questions in this chapter change a word into numbers. Think of Mary, who runs a shop in Shillong and writes her prices in a secret way so customers cannot read them. The trick is the same every time: write the position of each letter of the example word, put it next to the code, and see what was done to the positions.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 218' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='150' y='0' width='160' height='34' rx='7' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230' y='23' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>FACE = F6 A1 C3 E5</text><rect x='20' y='44' width='220' height='26' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='30' y='62' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Position of each letter</text><rect x='246' y='44' width='194' height='26' rx='5' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='343' y='62' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>6, 1, 3, 5</text><rect x='20' y='74' width='220' height='26' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='30' y='92' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Sum of positions</text><rect x='246' y='74' width='194' height='26' rx='5' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='343' y='92' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>6 + 1 + 3 + 5 = 15</text><rect x='20' y='104' width='220' height='26' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='30' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Position + 2</text><rect x='246' y='104' width='194' height='26' rx='5' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='343' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>8, 3, 5, 7</text><rect x='20' y='134' width='220' height='26' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='30' y='152' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Square of position</text><rect x='246' y='134' width='194' height='26' rx='5' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='343' y='152' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>36, 1, 9, 25</text><rect x='20' y='164' width='220' height='26' rx='5' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='30' y='182' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Positions in reverse order</text><rect x='246' y='164' width='194' height='26' rx='5' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='343' y='182' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>5, 3, 1, 6</text><text x='230' y='210' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>One word, five different number codes. The example tells you which one.</text></svg></div>
<p>Look at the size of the code first. A single number (like 73) is usually the <b>sum</b> of positions. A list of numbers the same length as the word is a list of positions, perhaps changed by +n, squared or written backwards. Very long codes, with numbers much bigger than 26, often mean <b>squares</b>.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> If FADE is coded as 7, 2, 5, 6, how is BEAD coded?<p>F6 A1 D4 E5 became 7, 2, 5, 6. Every position went up by 1. BEAD: B2 E5 A1 D4 becomes <b>3, 6, 2, 5</b>.</p></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> in a "reverse order" code, the numbers are the plain positions, only their order is reversed. Students who see the last letter first often think it is a new rule. Read the code from the end and it matches the word from the start.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>If ACE is coded as 9, how is BED coded?</li><li>If PEN is coded as 14, 5, 16, how is CUP coded?</li><li>If CAT is coded as 9, 1, 400, how is BAD coded?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Sum of positions: 2 + 5 + 4 = 11 · 2. Positions in reverse order: P16, U21, C3 → 16, 21, 3 · 3. Squares: 4, 1, 16</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">16.4</span>Each letter has its own digit</h2>
<p>In some codes there is no maths at all. Each letter is simply given a digit, like seat numbers given to passengers. You find each letter's digit by putting the word and the code side by side, letter under digit.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> If TAP is coded as 728 and PEN as 853, how is NAP coded?<p>From TAP: T = 7, A = 2, P = 8. From PEN: P = 8 (the same, good), E = 5, N = 3. So NAP = N3, A2, P8 = <b>328</b>.</p></div>
<div class="rule">Write a small key: letter = digit<small>A letter that appears in both words must get the same digit both times. Use it as a check.</small></div>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#F2A541;color:#17423B">A</div><div class="bub"><b>Ayesha:</b> Must I write the whole key every time?</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> Rarely. Read the options first. If only one starts with the right digit, you are done.</div></div></div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>If MILK is coded as 4672 and LIME as 7649, how is MILE coded?</li><li>Using the same code, how is LIKE coded?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. M4, I6, L7, K2, E9 → MILE = 4679 · 2. L7, I6, K2, E9 → 7629</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">16.5</span>Codes for whole words</h2>
<p>Now the codes are for whole words in short sentences, and the digits are mixed up, not in the same order as the words. The method is like finding which friend ate the last laddoo: see who was present every time a laddoo went missing.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 144' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='10' y='8' width='250' height='30' rx='6' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='22' y='28' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>green tea please</text><rect x='290' y='8' width='100' height='30' rx='6' fill='#17423B'/><text x='340' y='29' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>248</text><text x='275' y='28' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>=</text><rect x='10' y='46' width='250' height='30' rx='6' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='22' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>please come home</text><rect x='290' y='46' width='100' height='30' rx='6' fill='#17423B'/><text x='340' y='67' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>925</text><text x='275' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>=</text><rect x='10' y='84' width='250' height='30' rx='6' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='22' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>tea at home</text><rect x='290' y='84' width='100' height='30' rx='6' fill='#17423B'/><text x='340' y='105' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>586</text><text x='275' y='104' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>=</text><path d='M398,23 Q440,61 398,99' fill='none' stroke='#D95D39' stroke-width='2'/><text x='446' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='end'>tea</text><text x='446' y='80' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='end'>= 8</text><text x='230' y='136' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>"tea" is in sentences 1 and 3. The only digit in both codes is 8, so tea = 8.</text></svg></div>
<p>Do the same for every word. "please" is in sentences 1 and 2, and the only digit in both 248 and 925 is 2, so please = 2. "home" is in sentences 2 and 3, and the common digit of 925 and 586 is 5, so home = 5. Whatever is left over in a sentence belongs to the remaining word: green = 4, come = 9, at = 6.</p>
<div class="rule">Word common to two sentences = digit common to their two codes</div>
<p><b>Name-swap questions.</b> Another type renames things: "If pen is called book, book is called bag, and bag is called box, what do you carry books in?" First answer in real life: you carry books in a bag. Then give its code name: a bag is called <b>box</b>.</p>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> in name-swap questions, the answer is the code name, not the real thing. "Bag" is the real answer and it is usually one of the options to catch you.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>In the sentences above, what is the code for "come"?</li><li>If pen is called book, book is called bag, and bag is called box, what do you write with?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. come = 9 · 2. You write with a pen, and a pen is called book</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">16.6</span>How to solve any coding question</h2>
<div class="steps3"><div><b>1. Write positions</b>Put the number under every letter of the example word.</div><div><b>2. Spot the rule</b>Same letters? Shift? Opposite? Sum? Squares? Reversed? One digit each?</div><div><b>3. Apply and eliminate</b>Code the new word letter by letter and cross out options early.</div></div>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 300' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='65' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='395' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='48' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230' y='147.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>CODING</text><text x='230' y='164.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>DECODING</text><rect x='8' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Shift + or −</text><text x='80' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>CAT → DBU</text><rect x='308' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Reverse</text><text x='380' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>TABLE → ELBAT</text><rect x='-7' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='65' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Opposite letters</text><text x='65' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>pair adds to 27</text><rect x='323' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='395' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Positions</text><text x='395' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>sum, +n, squares</text><rect x='8' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Fixed digits</text><text x='80' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>one digit per letter</text><rect x='308' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Word codes</text><text x='380' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>common word, digit</text></svg></div><p class="cap">The whole chapter on one page. Revise from here before the exam.</p>
<div class="card"><h4>Quick card</h4><table><tr><td>Shift</td><td>code letter − word letter = step</td></tr><tr><td>Reverse</td><td>same letters, backwards</td></tr><tr><td>Opposite letter</td><td>27 − position</td></tr><tr><td>One number</td><td>usually the sum of positions</td></tr><tr><td>Big numbers</td><td>try squares of positions</td></tr><tr><td>Sentence codes</td><td>common word = common digit</td></tr></table></div></div>$less$ where no = 16;
update public.course_chapters set lesson_html = $less$<div class="pB"><div class="opener"><div class="k">Part B - Reasoning · Chapter 17</div><h1>Relationships and Types of Reasoning</h1><div class="sub">Blood relations, coded relations and the four types of reasoning</div></div>
<p>At a wedding in Ludhiana, an elderly aunt points at a young man and says, "He is the son of my husband's only brother." Blood-relation questions are this kind of puzzle, and the second half of the chapter covers the four <b>types of reasoning</b>. You will meet 3 real past questions here, one on relations and two on reasoning.</p>
<div class="chal"><div class="chh">⏱ 10-second challenge</div><div>Pointing to a girl, Sunita says to her friend, "She is the daughter of my father’s only son." How is the girl related to Sunita?</div><div class="chw">Make a quick guess. The answer is in section 17.2.</div></div>
<div class="goals"><h4>By the end of this chapter you will be able to</h4><ul><li>draw a family tree from a set of statements;</li><li>decode long "pointing" sentences such as "my father’s only son";</li><li>solve coded relations such as "A + B means A is the mother of B";</li><li>count the men and women in a group correctly;</li><li>tell deductive, inductive, analogical and abductive reasoning apart, and judge arguments as valid, sound, strong or cogent.</li></ul></div>

<h2><span class="n">17.1</span>Draw the family tree</h2>
<p>Suppose a question says: "Thomas is Lissy's brother. Lissy is Ann's mother. How is Thomas related to Ann?" Holding three people in your head is hard. On paper it takes ten seconds. Use a square for a male and a circle for a female. Put one generation on one line, with parents above and children below.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 240' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='118' y1='31' x2='182' y2='31' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><line x1='118' y1='37' x2='182' y2='37' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><rect x='83' y='17' width='34' height='34' rx='3' fill='#17423B'/><text x='100' y='39' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>J</text><circle cx='200' cy='34' r='18' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='200' y='39' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>G</text><text x='100' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Joseph</text><text x='200' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Grace</text><line x1='150' y1='37' x2='150' y2='84' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><line x1='90' y1='84' x2='210' y2='84' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><line x1='90' y1='84' x2='90' y2='102' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><line x1='210' y1='84' x2='210' y2='102' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><rect x='73' y='103' width='34' height='34' rx='3' fill='#17423B'/><text x='90' y='125' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>T</text><circle cx='210' cy='120' r='18' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='210' y='125' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>L</text><text x='68' y='125' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='end'>Thomas</text><text x='234' y='125' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Lissy</text><line x1='210' y1='138' x2='210' y2='184' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='210' cy='202' r='18' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='210' y='207' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>A</text><text x='234' y='207' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Ann</text><path d='M90,140 Q110,204 188,204' fill='none' stroke='#D95D39' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/><text x='60' y='214' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Thomas is Ann’s</text><text x='60' y='228' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>maternal uncle</text><rect x='282' y='6' width='176' height='192' rx='8' fill='#EEF4F2'/><rect x='287' y='15' width='34' height='34' rx='3' fill='#17423B'/><text x='304' y='37' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>M</text><text x='330' y='37' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>male</text><circle cx='304' cy='74' r='18' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='304' y='79' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>F</text><text x='330' y='79' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>female</text><line x1='290' y1='109' x2='318' y2='109' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><line x1='290' y1='115' x2='318' y2='115' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><text x='330' y='117' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>married</text><line x1='290' y1='146' x2='318' y2='146' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><text x='330' y='151' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>brother / sister</text><line x1='304' y1='166' x2='304' y2='188' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6'/><text x='330' y='181' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>parent above child</text></svg></div>
<p>Thomas and Lissy are on the same line, so they are brother and sister. Ann is below Lissy. So Thomas is Ann's mother's brother, her <b>maternal uncle</b>.</p>
<div class="rule">One generation on one line: parents above, children below<small>Mother’s brother = maternal uncle · father’s sister = paternal aunt · brother’s or sister’s son = nephew, daughter = niece</small></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> do not decide a person’s gender from the name alone. Use only words such as son, daughter, brother, sister, husband, wife, he or she. If gender is not given, draw the person with a plain dot.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>X is Y’s father. Z is X’s mother. How is Z related to Y?</li><li>Imran’s father’s father has only one son. How is that son related to Imran?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Grandmother (father’s mother) · 2. He is Imran’s father</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">17.2</span>Long sentences: break them from the end</h2>
<p>In "pointing" questions, the whole relation is packed into one sentence. Pointing to a girl, Ravi says to his friend, "She is the daughter of my father's only son." Do not start from "she". Start from the end, closest to the speaker, and work back.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 150' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='10' y='6' width='440' height='34' rx='7' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='230' y='28' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>"She is the daughter of  my father’s only son."</text><line x1='240' y1='42' x2='398' y2='42' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='20' y='62' width='130' height='58' rx='7' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='85.0' y='82' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>start from the end</text><text x='85.0' y='106' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>father’s only son</text><rect x='170' y='62' width='120' height='58' rx='7' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='230.0' y='82' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>Ravi is male, so</text><text x='230.0' y='106' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Ravi himself</text><rect x='310' y='62' width='130' height='58' rx='7' fill='#17423B'/><text x='375.0' y='82' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#fff' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>the girl is the daughter of</text><text x='375.0' y='106' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Ravi’s daughter</text><line x1='152' y1='91' x2='166' y2='91' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><line x1='292' y1='91' x2='306' y2='91' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='230' y='142' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Break the sentence from the end, one relation at a time.</text></svg></div>
<p>"My father's only son" is Ravi himself, because Ravi is male and his father has only one son. The girl is the daughter of that person, so she is Ravi's <b>daughter</b>.</p>
<div class="rule">"My father’s only son" = me, if I am male = my brother, if I am female<small>The same works for "my mother’s only son"</small></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> check whether the speaker is a man or a woman. A woman cannot be her father’s son, so for her "my father’s only son" is her brother. The answer changes completely.</div>
<div class="rev"><b>⏱ Challenge answer:</b> Niece, not daughter. Sunita is a woman, so her father’s only son is her brother, and the girl is her brother’s daughter. From Ravi, a man, the same sentence meant his own daughter.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Introducing a man, Lalremruati says, "He is the only son of my mother’s mother." How is he related to Lalremruati?</li><li>Pointing to a photo, Abdul says to his friend, "Her mother is my father’s only daughter." How is the girl in the photo related to Abdul?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. The son of her mother’s mother is her mother’s brother: maternal uncle · 2. Abdul’s father’s only daughter is Abdul’s sister; her daughter is Abdul’s niece</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">17.3</span>Coded relations</h2>
<p>Some questions give each relation a symbol, like a secret family language. Read each symbol as a sentence, one pair of letters at a time.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> If A − B means A is the sister of B, and A × B means A is the father of B, what does P × Q − R mean?<p>P × Q: P is the father of Q. Q − R: Q is the sister of R. So Q and R have the same father, and P is the <b>father</b> of R too.</p></div>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#F2A541;color:#17423B">A</div><div class="bub"><b>Ayesha:</b> The × and − look like sums. Do I calculate anything?</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> Nothing. Draw as you read: P on top, Q below P, R beside Q. One look then gives the answer.</div></div></div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>If A + B means A is the mother of B, and A ÷ B means A is the son of B, what does P ÷ Q + R mean?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: P is Q’s son and Q is R’s mother. P and R have the same mother, and P is male: P is R’s brother</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">17.4</span>Counting men and women</h2>
<p>To count the males and females in a group, write M or F next to a name only when a word makes it clear.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> A and B are sisters. C is A’s husband. D is B’s son. How many males and females are there?<p>"Sisters" makes A and B female. "Husband" makes C male. "Son" makes D male. So there are <b>2 males and 2 females</b>.</p></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> "brothers" gives two males, but "siblings", "children" and "parent" give no gender at all. Someone only mentioned, such as "C’s father", may not be in the group. Count only the people named in it.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>P, Q, R and S are in a room. P is Q’s wife. R is P’s daughter. S is R’s brother. How many males and females are there?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: P, R female; Q, S male: 2 males and 2 females</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">17.5</span>Four types of reasoning</h2>
<p>We draw conclusions every day. The road is wet, so it probably rained. The exam asks you to name the kind of reasoning behind a conclusion.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 184' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='10' y='6' width='215' height='70' rx='8' fill='#17423B'/><text x='117' y='28' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Deductive</text><text x='117' y='48' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>general → particular</text><text x='117' y='67' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>conclusion: CERTAIN</text><rect x='235' y='6' width='215' height='70' rx='8' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='342' y='28' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Inductive</text><text x='342' y='48' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>particular → general</text><text x='342' y='67' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>conclusion: probable</text><rect x='10' y='84' width='215' height='70' rx='8' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='117' y='106' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Analogical</text><text x='117' y='126' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>similar case → this case</text><text x='117' y='145' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>conclusion: probable</text><rect x='235' y='84' width='215' height='70' rx='8' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='342' y='106' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Abductive</text><text x='342' y='126' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>observation → best explanation</text><text x='342' y='145' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>conclusion: probable</text><text x='230' y='176' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Only deduction promises a certain conclusion. The other three give a likely one.</text></svg></div>
<table class="data" style="break-inside: avoid"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Everyday example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Deductive</td><td>All trains on this route stop at Guwahati. My train is on this route. So my train will stop at Guwahati.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inductive</td><td>The 8 o’clock bus was late every day this month, so it is always late.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Analogical</td><td>This new phone is like my old one in many ways. My old one lasted five years, so this one may too.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Abductive</td><td>The milk smells sour, so it has probably gone bad. (The best explanation of what I notice.)</td></tr></table>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#17423B;color:#fff">T</div><div class="bub"><b>Tenzin:</b> These four names all sound alike to me. How do I tell them apart?</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> General rule to one case? Deductive. Only likely? Then ask what it rests on: many observations (inductive), one similar case (analogical), or explaining one observation (abductive).</div></div></div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>"All birds have feathers. A sparrow is a bird. So a sparrow has feathers."</li><li>"Every time I ate at that dhaba I fell ill, so all its food is bad."</li><li>"My car and my brother’s car are the same model. Mine gives 18 km per litre, so his probably does too."</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Deductive · 2. Inductive · 3. Analogical</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">17.6</span>Judging an argument</h2>
<p>A deductive argument promises certainty. It is <b>valid</b> when its form is right: if the premises were true, the conclusion would have to be true. The conclusion only spells out what was already inside the premises. A valid argument with all true premises is <b>sound</b>.</p>
<p>An inductive argument's conclusion goes beyond the premises, to cases we have not seen, so it is judged in degrees: <b>strong</b> if the premises make the conclusion very likely, <b>weak</b> if not. A strong argument with all true premises is <b>cogent</b>.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 196' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><text x='117' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Deductive argument</text><rect x='10' y='26' width='215' height='66' rx='8' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='117' y='48' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>VALID</text><text x='117' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>form is right: true premises</text><text x='117' y='81' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>would force a true conclusion</text><line x1='117' y1='95' x2='117' y2='113' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><rect x='10' y='117' width='215' height='50' rx='8' fill='#17423B'/><text x='117' y='138' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>SOUND</text><text x='117' y='157' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#FBE7C6' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>valid + all premises true</text><text x='342' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Inductive argument</text><rect x='235' y='26' width='215' height='66' rx='8' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='342' y='48' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>STRONG</text><text x='342' y='66' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>premises make the</text><text x='342' y='81' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>conclusion very likely</text><line x1='342' y1='95' x2='342' y2='113' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><rect x='235' y='117' width='215' height='50' rx='8' fill='#17423B'/><text x='342' y='138' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>COGENT</text><text x='342' y='157' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#FBE7C6' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>strong + all premises true</text><text x='230' y='188' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Wrong form: invalid (deductive) or weak (inductive).</text></svg></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> valid does not mean true. "All fish can fly. A shark is a fish. So a shark can fly." The form is valid, but the first premise is false, so the argument is not sound.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Is "All fish can fly. A shark is a fish. So a shark can fly." valid? Is it sound?</li><li>A strong inductive argument has one false premise. Is it cogent?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Valid, but not sound (a premise is false) · 2. No. Cogent needs strong + all premises true</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">17.7</span>How to solve these questions</h2>
<div class="steps3"><div><b>1. Draw</b>Square for male, circle for female, one generation per line.</div><div><b>2. Read from the end</b>Break long sentences and coded relations one link at a time.</div><div><b>3. Name the reasoning</b>Rule to case = deductive (certain). Otherwise probable: inductive, analogical or abductive.</div></div>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 300' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='65' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='395' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='48' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230' y='147.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>RELATIONS</text><text x='230' y='164.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>REASONING</text><rect x='8' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Draw the tree</text><text x='80' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>square male, circle female</text><rect x='308' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Long sentences</text><text x='380' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>break from the end</text><rect x='-7' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='65' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Coded relations</text><text x='65' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>decode symbol by symbol</text><rect x='323' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='395' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Count genders</text><text x='395' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>only clear clues</text><rect x='8' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Deduction</text><text x='80' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>certain; valid, sound</text><rect x='308' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Induction</text><text x='380' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>probable; strong, cogent</text></svg></div><p class="cap">The whole chapter on one page. Revise from here before the exam.</p>
<div class="card"><h4>Quick card</h4><table><tr><td>Symbols</td><td>square male, circle female, double line married</td></tr><tr><td>"My father’s only son"</td><td>me (male speaker) or my brother (female speaker)</td></tr><tr><td>Deductive</td><td>general → particular, certain; valid → sound</td></tr><tr><td>Inductive</td><td>particular → general, probable; strong → cogent</td></tr><tr><td>Analogical</td><td>similar case → this case, probable</td></tr><tr><td>Abductive</td><td>observation → best explanation, probable</td></tr></table></div></div>$less$ where no = 17;
update public.course_chapters set lesson_html = $less$<div class="pC"><div class="opener"><div class="k">Part C - Data Interpretation · Chapter 18</div><h1>Data Basics and Tables</h1><div class="sub">Sources and kinds of data, scales, governance, and reading a table</div></div>
<p>The ration shop records how much rice it gave out, the school records attendance, and your bank records every rupee you spend. All of this is data. Paper 1 asks about it in two ways: short theory questions on where data comes from and what kind it is, and a set of questions on a table. You will meet 5 real past questions in this chapter, all on one table.</p>
<div class="chal"><div class="chh">⏱ 10-second challenge</div><div>Bikash from Guwahati fills a college form. He writes his height (167 cm), the number of his cousins (6) and his roll number (47). All three are numbers. Which one is <b>not</b> quantitative data?</div><div class="chw">Make a quick guess. The answer is in section 18.2.</div></div>
<div class="goals"><h4>By the end of this chapter you will be able to</h4><ul><li>tell primary data from secondary data, and name the ways of collecting data;</li><li>classify data as nominal, ordinal, discrete or continuous;</li><li>name the four measurement scales and give an example of each;</li><li>recall the main data governance terms (data.gov.in, NDSAP, DPDP Act);</li><li>read a table carefully and use the five DI calculations, with the right base for "more than" and "less than".</li></ul></div>


<h2><span class="n">18.1</span>Where does data come from?</h2>
<p>Lalremruati is a researcher in Aizawl. In March she visits villages and interviews 200 farmers herself. In April she also uses Census 2011 figures from a government report. Both are data, but they are not the same kind.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 162' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='222' height='130' rx='8' fill='#17423B'/><rect x='238' y='0' width='222' height='130' rx='8' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='111' y='26' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>PRIMARY data</text><text x='349' y='26' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='15' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>SECONDARY data</text><text x='111' y='48' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#FBE7C6' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>you collect it yourself,</text><text x='111' y='64' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#FBE7C6' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>for the first time</text><text x='349' y='48' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>someone else collected it</text><text x='349' y='64' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>earlier; you reuse it</text><text x='111' y='90' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>• survey, interview</text><text x='111' y='105' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>• experiment</text><text x='111' y='120' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>• observation</text><text x='349' y='90' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>• Census reports</text><text x='349' y='105' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>• NSSO reports</text><text x='349' y='120' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>• published statistics</text><text x='230' y='154' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Ask one question: who collected it, and for whose study?</text></svg></div>
<p>The interviews are <b>primary data</b>: Lalremruati collected them first-hand, for her own study. The Census figures are <b>secondary data</b> for her: the government collected them earlier, and she is reusing them.</p>
<p><b>Ways of collecting data.</b> A <b>census</b> covers every unit (every household in the country). A <b>sample survey</b> covers only a part (500 households chosen from a city). Data can also come from <b>observation</b>, <b>experiments</b> and <b>administrative records</b>, such as school admission registers or hospital records.</p>
<div class="rule">Primary = collected first-hand for this study.   Secondary = collected earlier by someone else</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Teresa, a teacher in Kochi, records her own students’ marks for her study. Primary or secondary?</li><li>Imran uses figures from a published RBI report. Primary or secondary?</li><li>Asking 500 voters out of 50,000 in a Ludhiana ward: census or sample survey?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Primary · 2. Secondary · 3. Sample survey</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">18.2</span>What kind of data is it?</h2>
<p>Think of a class register. It has each student's name, gender, rank, number of siblings and height. Some of these are categories, some are numbers. The exam asks you to sort them.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 182' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='180' y='4' width='100' height='30' rx='15.0' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230.0' y='23.9' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>DATA</text><path d='M230,34 L230,46 M115,46 L345,46 M115,46 L115,58 M345,46 L345,58' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6' fill='none'/><rect x='25' y='58' width='180' height='30' rx='15.0' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='115.0' y='77.2' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Qualitative (categories)</text><rect x='255' y='58' width='180' height='30' rx='15.0' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='345.0' y='77.2' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Quantitative (numbers)</text><path d='M115,88 L115,100 M60,100 L170,100 M60,100 L60,110 M170,100 L170,110 M345,88 L345,100 M290,100 L400,100 M290,100 L290,110 M400,100 L400,110' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.6' fill='none'/><rect x='6' y='110' width='108' height='66' rx='7' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='60' y='130' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Nominal</text><text x='60' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>no order</text><text x='60' y='166' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>gender, state</text><rect x='116' y='110' width='108' height='66' rx='7' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='170' y='130' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Ordinal</text><text x='170' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>has an order</text><text x='170' y='166' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>rank, grade</text><rect x='236' y='110' width='108' height='66' rx='7' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='290' y='130' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Discrete</text><text x='290' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>counted</text><text x='290' y='166' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>number of children</text><rect x='346' y='110' width='108' height='66' rx='7' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='400' y='130' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Continuous</text><text x='400' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>measured</text><text x='400' y='166' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='10.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>height, time</text></svg></div>
<p><b>Qualitative</b> data are categories. If the categories have no order (male, female; Rajasthan, Kerala), they are <b>nominal</b>. If they have an order (first, second, third; grade A, B, C), they are <b>ordinal</b>. <b>Quantitative</b> data are amounts. If you count them in whole numbers (3 children), they are <b>discrete</b>. If you measure them and they can take any value (152.4 cm), they are <b>continuous</b>.</p>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> a number is not always quantitative. A PIN code or a roll number is only a label. You cannot add two PIN codes, so they are nominal.</div>
<div class="rev"><b>⏱ Challenge answer:</b> the roll number. Bikash&#39;s 47 only names him in the register, so it is nominal. His height is continuous and his number of cousins is discrete.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Blood group</li><li>Weight of a baby</li><li>Number of rooms in a house</li><li>Customer rating: poor, good, excellent</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Nominal · 2. Continuous · 3. Discrete · 4. Ordinal</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">18.3</span>The four measurement scales</h2>
<p>The four scales are like four steps of a ladder. Each step lets you do everything the step below allows, plus one more thing.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 218' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='10' y='172' width='440' height='40' rx='6' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='22' y='197' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Nominal</text><text x='102' y='197' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>names or labels only</text><text x='444' y='197' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='end'>PIN code, blood group</text><rect x='32' y='126' width='418' height='40' rx='6' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='44' y='151' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Ordinal</text><text x='124' y='151' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>+ an order</text><text x='444' y='151' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='end'>rank, grade A/B/C</text><rect x='54' y='80' width='396' height='40' rx='6' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='66' y='105' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Interval</text><text x='146' y='105' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>+ equal gaps, no true zero</text><text x='444' y='105' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='end'>temperature in °C</text><rect x='76' y='34' width='374' height='40' rx='6' fill='#17423B'/><text x='88' y='59' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Ratio</text><text x='168' y='59' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#fff' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='start'>+ a true zero</text><text x='444' y='59' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='end'>weight, income</text><text x='10' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>Each step up keeps everything below it and adds one more thing.</text></svg></div>
<p>On a <b>nominal</b> scale you can only name things. On an <b>ordinal</b> scale you can also put them in order, but the gaps are not equal: the gap between rank 1 and rank 2 may be 1 mark or 20 marks. On an <b>interval</b> scale the gaps are equal, but zero is not a real "nothing": 0°C does not mean there is no temperature. On a <b>ratio</b> scale zero means "nothing at all" (0 kg, ₹0), so you can say one value is twice another.</p>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> 20°C is not "twice as hot" as 10°C, because 0°C is not a true zero. You can say that about 20 kg and 10 kg, because weight is on a ratio scale.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Monthly income</li><li>Shirt sizes S, M, L, XL</li><li>Temperature in °C</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Ratio · 2. Ordinal · 3. Interval</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">18.4</span>Data and governance</h2>
<p>Governments collect a huge amount of data. Making it open helps citizens and researchers, and protecting personal data keeps people safe. Remember these three names:</p>
<table class="data" style="break-inside: avoid"><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>data.gov.in</b></td><td>The Open Government Data (OGD) Platform India</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>NDSAP, 2012</b></td><td>National Data Sharing and Accessibility Policy</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>DPDP Act, 2023</b></td><td>Digital Personal Data Protection Act</td></tr></table>
<p>Good data helps in evidence-based policy, transparency, targeting welfare schemes to the right people, and monitoring whether a scheme is working.</p>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>What does DPDP stand for, and in which year was the Act passed?</li><li>In which year did the NDSAP come?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Digital Personal Data Protection, 2023 · 2. 2012</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">18.5</span>Reading a table</h2>
<p>Many mistakes in table questions are reading mistakes, not maths mistakes. A student picks the wrong row, or forgets that the figures are in thousands. Read a table the way you read a train timetable: find the right row, then the right column, then put your finger where they meet.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 200' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><text x='230' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Books sold by four shops (number of books)</text><rect x='230' y='26' width='70' height='130' rx='0' fill='#FBE7C6'/><rect x='90' y='78' width='280' height='26' rx='0' fill='#FBE7C6'/><rect x='90' y='26' width='70' height='26' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='125.0' y='44' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Shop</text><rect x='160' y='26' width='70' height='26' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='195.0' y='44' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Jan</text><rect x='230' y='26' width='70' height='26' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='265.0' y='44' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Feb</text><rect x='300' y='26' width='70' height='26' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='335.0' y='44' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Mar</text><rect x='90' y='52' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='125.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>P</text><rect x='160' y='52' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='195.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>120</text><rect x='230' y='52' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='265.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>150</text><rect x='300' y='52' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='335.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>180</text><rect x='90' y='78' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='125.0' y='96' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Q</text><rect x='160' y='78' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='195.0' y='96' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>90</text><rect x='230' y='78' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='265.0' y='96' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>135</text><rect x='300' y='78' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='335.0' y='96' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>108</text><rect x='90' y='104' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='125.0' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>R</text><rect x='160' y='104' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='195.0' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>200</text><rect x='230' y='104' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='265.0' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>160</text><rect x='300' y='104' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='335.0' y='122' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>240</text><rect x='90' y='130' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='125.0' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>S</text><rect x='160' y='130' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='195.0' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>75</text><rect x='230' y='130' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='265.0' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>100</text><rect x='300' y='130' width='70' height='26' fill='none' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='335.0' y='148' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>125</text><circle cx='265.0' cy='91.0' r='17' fill='none' stroke='#D95D39' stroke-width='2.2'/><text x='380' y='95.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>← row Q</text><text x='265.0' y='174' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>↑ column Feb</text><text x='230' y='196' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>Shop Q in February: run along the row and down the column. Value 135.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Title and units → row and column → write the values down<small>Only then start calculating</small></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> check the units in the title or the column heads: thousands, lakh, crore, %, ₹. If the answer options are in lakh and the table is in thousands, you must convert.</div>

<h2><span class="n">18.6</span>The five DI calculations</h2>
<p>Almost every table question asks for one of five things. Here they are, all worked on the books table in the picture above.</p>
<table class="data"><tr><th>Question says</th><th>Do this</th><th>On the books table</th></tr>
<tr><td>Difference of X and Y</td><td>X − Y</td><td>R in Mar − Q in Jan = 240 − 90 = 150</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ratio of X to Y</td><td>X : Y, then simplify</td><td>Q Feb : R Feb = 135 : 160 = 27 : 32</td></tr>
<tr><td>Average of ...</td><td>sum ÷ count</td><td>R: (200 + 160 + 240) ÷ 3 = 200</td></tr>
<tr><td>X is what % of Y?</td><td>X ÷ Y × 100</td><td>S Jan of R Feb: 75 ÷ 160 × 100 = 46.875%</td></tr>
<tr><td>X is what % more or less than Y?</td><td>(X − Y) ÷ <b>Y</b> × 100</td><td>R Mar more than P Mar: 60 ÷ 180 × 100 = 33.33%</td></tr></table>
<p>To simplify 135 : 160, divide both by 5 to get 27 : 32. Nothing else divides both, so stop there.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 154' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='40' y='30.0' width='80' height='90.0' rx='4' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='24.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>P (Mar) 180</text><rect x='150' y='0.0' width='80' height='120.0' rx='4' fill='#17423B'/><text x='190' y='-6.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>R (Mar) 240</text><line x1='30' y1='120' x2='245' y2='120' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><line x1='150' y1='30.0' x2='230' y2='30.0' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='2' stroke-dasharray='4 3'/><text x='190' y='19.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>gap 60</text><rect x='262' y='14' width='192' height='50' rx='7' fill='#FBE7C6'/><text x='358' y='34' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>R is ?% MORE than P</text><text x='358' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>60 ÷ 180 × 100 = 33.33%</text><rect x='262' y='74' width='192' height='50' rx='7' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='358' y='94' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>P is ?% LESS than R</text><text x='358' y='114' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>60 ÷ 240 × 100 = 25%</text><text x='230' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Same gap, different answers. Divide by the value that comes after "than".</text></svg></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> in "what % more than" and "what % less than", the base is always the value after "than". The same gap of 60 gives 33.33% on one side and 25% on the other, and both numbers are usually among the options.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Average sales of shop Q over the three months?</li><li>Ratio of S in March to P in February?</li><li>Q’s February sales are what % more than its January sales?</li><li>S in January is what % of P in January?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. (90 + 135 + 108) ÷ 3 = 333 ÷ 3 = 111 · 2. 125 : 150 = 5 : 6 · 3. 45 ÷ 90 × 100 = 50% · 4. 75 ÷ 120 × 100 = 62.5%</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">18.7</span>Approximation saves time</h2>
<p>The word "approximately" in a question is a signal: you are allowed to round. Options are usually far apart, so a rough answer is enough to pick the right one.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> 487 is approximately what percent of 1,212? Options: 30%, 40%, 50%, 60%.<p>Round to two important digits: 487 is about 490 and 1,212 is about 1,200. 490 ÷ 1,200 is a little over 0.4, so the answer is <b>40%</b>. (The exact value is 40.18%.)</p></div>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#F2A541;color:#17423B">A</div><div class="bub"><b>Ayesha:</b> So I can round every number and finish fast?</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> Yes, when the options are far apart. If two options are close, go back and calculate more exactly.</div></div></div>

<h2><span class="n">18.8</span>How to solve any table question</h2>
<div class="steps3"><div><b>1. Read the frame</b>Title, units, rows and columns. Note any "in thousands".</div><div><b>2. Pick the values</b>Find each value with your finger and write it down.</div><div><b>3. Choose the formula</b>Which of the five? For "more or less than", divide by the "than" value.</div></div>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 300' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='65' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='395' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='48' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230' y='147.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>DATA AND</text><text x='230' y='164.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>TABLES</text><rect x='8' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Source</text><text x='80' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>primary or secondary</text><rect x='308' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Kind</text><text x='380' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>qualitative or quantitative</text><rect x='-7' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='65' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Scale</text><text x='65' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>nom, ord, int, ratio</text><rect x='323' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='395' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Governance</text><text x='395' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>data.gov.in, DPDP</text><rect x='8' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Read the table</text><text x='80' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>units, row, column</text><rect x='308' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Five calculations</text><text x='380' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>base after "than"</text></svg></div><p class="cap">The whole chapter on one page. Revise from here before the exam.</p>
<div class="card"><h4>Quick card</h4><table><tr><td>Primary / secondary</td><td>collected first-hand / reused from others</td></tr><tr><td>Nominal, ordinal</td><td>categories without / with order</td></tr><tr><td>Discrete, continuous</td><td>counted / measured</td></tr><tr><td>Interval vs ratio</td><td>no true zero (°C) / true zero (kg)</td></tr><tr><td>Governance</td><td>data.gov.in, NDSAP 2012, DPDP Act 2023</td></tr><tr><td>% more / less than Y</td><td>(X − Y) ÷ Y × 100</td></tr></table></div></div>$less$ where no = 18;
update public.course_chapters set lesson_html = $less$<div class="pC"><div class="opener"><div class="k">Part C - Data Interpretation · Chapter 19</div><h1>Percentage Tables</h1><div class="sub">Tables that give shares instead of amounts</div></div>
<p>A family planning its month often thinks in shares: about a quarter for rent, a little more for food, something kept aside for savings. A percentage table works the same way. It gives each item's share of a total and leaves the rupees for you to work out. You will meet 10 real past questions in this chapter, all built on two percentage tables.</p>
<div class="chal"><div class="chh">⏱ 10-second challenge</div><div>Farhan runs a bakery in Pune. In January his sales were ₹1,00,000 and 20% of that came from cakes. In February his sales were ₹1,50,000 and only 15% came from cakes. Did his cake sales fall?</div><div class="chw">Make a quick guess. The answer is in section 19.4.</div></div>
<div class="goals"><h4>By the end of this chapter you will be able to</h4><ul><li>find the amount of any item when the total is given;</li><li>find the total when only one item's amount is given;</li><li>use the ratio shortcut and skip the total altogether;</li><li>compare two years correctly when the totals are different;</li><li>choose the right calculation for "difference", "ratio", "average" and "% more than".</li></ul></div>


<h2><span class="n">19.1</span>A table of shares, not amounts</h2>
<p>Gurpreet's family in Ludhiana earns ₹50,000 a month. She writes down how it is used: Rent 25%, Food 30%, Education 15%, Travel 10%, Savings 20%. Notice that these add up to 100%. The whole income is cut into shares, and every share is a piece of the <b>same</b> ₹50,000.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 134' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><text x='230' y='14' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Total income ₹50,000 = 100%</text><rect x='30' y='44' width='100' height='38' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80.0' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>25%</text><text x='80.0' y='38' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Rent</text><rect x='130' y='44' width='120' height='38' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='190.0' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>30%</text><text x='190.0' y='38' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Food</text><rect x='250' y='44' width='60' height='38' rx='0' fill='#FBE7C6' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='280.0' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>15%</text><text x='280.0' y='38' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Education</text><rect x='310' y='44' width='40' height='38' rx='0' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='330.0' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>10%</text><text x='330.0' y='38' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Travel</text><rect x='350' y='44' width='80' height='38' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='390.0' y='68' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>20%</text><text x='390.0' y='38' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Savings</text><path d='M30,88 L30,94 L130,94 L130,88' fill='none' stroke='#D95D39' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='110' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Rent = 25% of 50,000</text><text x='80' y='125' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>= ₹12,500</text><text x='330' y='110' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>The table gives only the shares.</text><text x='330' y='125' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>Money = share × total.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Amount of an item = its % × total ÷ 100<small>Rent = 25 × 50,000 ÷ 100 = ₹12,500</small></div>
<p>You already know the quick way from Chapter 3: find 1% first. Here 1% of 50,000 is ₹500, so 25% is 25 × 500 = ₹12,500.</p>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>How much does Gurpreet's family spend on Food?</li><li>How much do Education and Travel take together?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 30 × 500 = ₹15,000 · 2. 15% + 10% = 25%, so 25 × 500 = ₹12,500</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">19.2</span>Only one amount is given: find the total</h2>
<p>Often the question does not tell you the total. It gives you the amount of <b>one</b> item and leaves you to find the rest. Suppose Joseph and Mary in Kochi plan their wedding with this budget, and they tell you the caterer was paid ₹90,000.</p>
<table class="data"><tr><th>Head</th><th>Catering</th><th>Decoration</th><th>Clothes</th><th>Jewellery</th><th>Photos</th><th>Others</th></tr><tr><td>Share</td><td>30%</td><td>12%</td><td>25%</td><td>20%</td><td>8%</td><td>5%</td></tr></table>
<p>Catering is 30% of the budget. So 30% of the total is ₹90,000. Let us work backwards, one per cent at a time.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 118' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='8' y='26' width='128' height='58' rx='7' fill='#EEF4F2'/><text x='72.0' y='46' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>Catering 30%</text><text x='72.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>₹90,000</text><rect x='166' y='26' width='128' height='58' rx='7' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='230.0' y='46' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>÷ 30</text><text x='230.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>1% = ₹3,000</text><rect x='324' y='26' width='128' height='58' rx='7' fill='#17423B'/><text x='388.0' y='46' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#fff' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>× 100</text><text x='388.0' y='70' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='19' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>₹3,00,000</text><line x1='138' y1='55' x2='162' y2='55' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><line x1='296' y1='55' x2='320' y2='55' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='230' y='16' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>one known piece  →  one per cent  →  the whole</text><text x='230' y='110' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Once you know 1%, any other share is easy: 12% = 12 × 3,000.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Known amount ÷ its % = 1% of the total. Then multiply by 100 for the total.<small>90,000 ÷ 30 = 3,000, so the total is 3,000 × 100 = ₹3,00,000</small></div>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> How much was spent on Decoration (12%)?<p>1% = ₹3,000, so 12% = 12 × 3,000 = <b>₹36,000</b>.</p></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> the ₹90,000 is not the total. Some students take 30% of 90,000 and get ₹27,000. Ask yourself: is the number I was given the whole, or a piece? Here it is a piece, so you divide by its %, you do not multiply.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>In a Shillong school's yearly expenses, Salaries are 45% and cost ₹5,40,000. Find the total expenses.</li><li>In the same school, the Library is 5% of the expenses. How much is that?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 5,40,000 ÷ 45 = 12,000 = 1%, total = ₹12,00,000 · 2. 5 × 12,000 = ₹60,000</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">19.3</span>The ratio shortcut: skip the total</h2>
<p>Look again at the wedding table. We found the total only to reach Decoration. Is there a faster road? Yes. Catering and Decoration are pieces of the same total, so their amounts stand in the same ratio as their percentages, 30 : 12.</p>
<div class="rule">Same total: amount of B = amount of A × (% of B) ÷ (% of A)<small>Decoration = 90,000 × <span class="fr"><span>12</span><span>30</span></span> = ₹36,000, the same answer in one line</small></div>
<div class="chat"><div class="cb"><div class="av" style="background:#17423B;color:#fff">T</div><div class="bub"><b>Tenzin:</b> Do I need to work out what Jewellery and Others cost together?</div></div><div class="cb right"><div class="av" style="background:#5b6b6f;color:#fff">✎</div><div class="bub"><b>Teacher:</b> No. Together they are 20% + 5% = 25%, the same share as Clothes. Equal pieces of one total cost the same money.</div></div></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> this shortcut works only inside one total, for example one year or one event. If the two percentages belong to two different years with two different totals, the shortcut gives a wrong answer. That is the next section.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Catering cost ₹90,000. Use the shortcut to find Clothes (25%).</li><li>In a different wedding with the same shares, the Photos (8%) cost ₹40,000. How much did the Jewellery (20%) cost?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 90,000 × <span class="fr"><span>25</span><span>30</span></span> = ₹75,000 · 2. 40,000 × <span class="fr"><span>20</span><span>8</span></span> = ₹1,00,000</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">19.4</span>Two years, two different totals</h2>
<p>Now a very common mistake. A newspaper sells in four towns. The table gives each town's share of readers in 2022 and in 2024, and the total readers in each year.</p>
<table class="data"><tr><th>Town</th><th>2022 (%)</th><th>2024 (%)</th></tr><tr><td>P</td><td>20</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Q</td><td>25</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>R</td><td>30</td><td>35</td></tr><tr><td>S</td><td>25</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Total readers</td><td>4,00,000</td><td>6,00,000</td></tr></table>
<p>Town P's share fell from 20% to 15%. Did P lose readers? Many students say yes at once. But the total grew from 4 lakh to 6 lakh. Let us find the actual numbers.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 160' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><text x='10' y='51' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>2022</text><rect x='52' y='28' width='240' height='34' rx='4' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='52' y='28' width='48.0' height='34' rx='4' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='76.0' y='50' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>P 20%</text><text x='108.0' y='50' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>P = 80,000</text><text x='286' y='50' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='end'>total 4 lakh</text><text x='10' y='115' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>2024</text><rect x='52' y='92' width='360' height='34' rx='4' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='52' y='92' width='54.0' height='34' rx='4' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='79.0' y='114' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>P 15%</text><text x='114.0' y='114' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>P = 90,000</text><text x='406' y='114' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='end'>total 6 lakh</text><text x='230' y='150' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>P's share fell from 20% to 15%, yet P's readers rose: 80,000 → 90,000.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Different totals: change each % into a number first, then compare<small>A percentage only means something next to its own total</small></div>
<div class="rev"><b>⏱ Challenge answer:</b> no, they rose. January: 20% of 1,00,000 = ₹20,000. February: 15% of 1,50,000 = ₹22,500. The share fell, but the total grew more.</div>
<p>Often the totals are not printed, and you get one town's readers in each year instead. Then you simply use section 19.2 twice, once for each year.</p>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> In 2022, Town Q (25%) had 1,00,000 readers. In 2024, Town Q (20%) had 1,20,000 readers. Find P's readers in both years.<p>2022: 1% = 1,00,000 ÷ 25 = 4,000, so P (20%) = 80,000. 2024: 1% = 1,20,000 ÷ 20 = 6,000, so P (15%) = <b>90,000</b>. P's readers went up by 10,000.</p></div>
<div class='think'><b>💡 Think of it like this:</b> sometimes the question gives only a ratio, for example &quot;P's readers in 2022 and 2024 were in the ratio 8 : 9&quot;. Write it as 20% of the first total : 15% of the second total = 8 : 9. So the first total : second total = <span class="fr"><span>8</span><span>20</span></span> : <span class="fr"><span>9</span><span>15</span></span> = 0.4 : 0.6 = 2 : 3. That matches 4 lakh : 6 lakh.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Find Town R's readers in 2022 and in 2024. By what % did they rise?</li><li>What is the difference between Town S's readers in 2024 and in 2022?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 30% of 4,00,000 = 1,20,000 and 35% of 6,00,000 = 2,10,000; rise 90,000 ÷ 1,20,000 = 75% · 2. 1,80,000 − 1,00,000 = 80,000</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">19.5</span>The five calculations, and the word "than"</h2>
<p>Once you have the numbers, almost every DI question asks for one of five things. You met each of them in Part A. Here they are in one place.</p>
<table class="data"><tr><th>The question says</th><th>You do</th></tr><tr><td>"Difference between X and Y"</td><td>X − Y</td></tr><tr><td>"Ratio of X to Y"</td><td>X : Y, then simplify</td></tr><tr><td>"Average of ..."</td><td>sum ÷ how many</td></tr><tr><td>"X is what % of Y?"</td><td>X ÷ Y × 100</td></tr><tr><td>"X is what % more/less than Y?"</td><td>(X − Y) ÷ <b>Y</b> × 100</td></tr></table>
<p>Only the last one needs care. Sana earns ₹50 a day from her stall in Patna and Ravi earns ₹40. The gap is ₹10. But ₹10 is a bigger share of 40 than of 50, so "more than Ravi" and "less than Sana" give two different answers.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 154' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><rect x='90' y='48.0' width='80' height='64.0' rx='4' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='130' y='128' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Ravi ₹40</text><rect x='230' y='32.0' width='80' height='80.0' rx='4' fill='#17423B' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='270' y='128' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Sana ₹50</text><rect x='230' y='32' width='80' height='16' rx='0' fill='#D95D39'/><text x='270' y='44' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>10</text><line x1='60' y1='112' x2='340' y2='112' stroke='#9fb3ae'/><text x='330' y='30' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>"more than Ravi"</text><text x='330' y='46' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>10 ÷ 40 = 25%</text><text x='330' y='74' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>"less than Sana"</text><text x='330' y='90' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#D95D39' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='start'>10 ÷ 50 = 20%</text><text x='230' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Same gap of 10, two answers. Divide by the value written after "than".</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">"% more than Y" or "% less than Y": divide the gap by Y, the value after "than"</div>
<div class='think'><b>💡 Think of it like this:</b> when the question says "approximately", it is allowing you to round. The options are usually far apart. For 7,950 ÷ 24,100, think 8,000 ÷ 24,000 = 1/3, about 33%. The exact answer is 32.99%, so nothing is lost.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>From the towns table, find the ratio of Q's readers to R's readers in 2024.</li><li>P had 90,000 readers in 2024 and 80,000 in 2022. In 2022, P had what % fewer readers than in 2024?</li><li>1,20,000 is what % more than 90,000?</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. Same year, so use the %: 20 : 35 = 4 : 7 · 2. 10,000 ÷ 90,000 ≈ 11.1% · 3. 30,000 ÷ 90,000 = 33⅓%</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">19.6</span>How to solve any percentage-table question</h2>
<div class="steps3"><div><b>1. Same total?</b>One year or one event: work with the % directly. Two years: get numbers first.</div><div><b>2. Find 1%</b>From the total, or from the one amount given. Or use the ratio shortcut.</div><div><b>3. Pick the calculation</b>Difference, ratio, average, % of, or % more. Watch the word "than".</div></div>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 300' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='40' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='65' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='395' y2='150' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='80' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='150' x2='380' y2='260' stroke='#F2A541' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='48' fill='#17423B'/><text x='230' y='147.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>PERCENTAGE</text><text x='230' y='164.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='14' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>TABLES</text><rect x='8' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Value</text><text x='80' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>share × total</text><rect x='308' y='16' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='36' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Total from a part</text><text x='380' y='54' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>find 1% first</text><rect x='-7' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='65' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Ratio shortcut</text><text x='65' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>same total: use %</text><rect x='323' y='126' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='395' y='146' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Two years</text><text x='395' y='164' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>numbers first</text><rect x='8' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='80' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>% more than</text><text x='80' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>divide by "than"</text><rect x='308' y='236' width='144' height='48' rx='10' fill='#fff' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='380' y='256' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Round</text><text x='380' y='274' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>"approximately"</text></svg></div><p class="cap">The whole chapter on one page. Revise from here before the exam.</p>
<div class="card"><h4>Quick card</h4><table><tr><td>Amount of an item</td><td>% × total ÷ 100</td></tr><tr><td>Total from one item</td><td>amount ÷ its % = 1%; then × 100</td></tr><tr><td>Same total</td><td>amount B = amount A × %B ÷ %A</td></tr><tr><td>Same % in one year</td><td>same amount</td></tr><tr><td>Two different totals</td><td>turn each % into a number first</td></tr><tr><td>% more / less than Y</td><td>gap ÷ Y × 100</td></tr></table></div></div>$less$ where no = 19;
update public.course_chapters set lesson_html = $less$<div class="pC"><div class="opener"><div class="k">Part C - Data Interpretation · Chapter 20</div><h1>Two-Part Tables, Charts and Timed Sets</h1><div class="sub">Totals with shares, profit tables, charts and speed</div></div>
<p>A school notice board says: "640 students passed this year, and 40% of them were girls." That one line holds a total and a share of it, and many DI tables are built just like it. Others give a company's income and profit %, or show the data as a chart. You will meet 25 real past questions here, all on tables; charts are included so that a pie or a bar chart never surprises you.</p>
<div class="chal"><div class="chh">⏱ 10-second challenge</div><div>Harpreet&#39;s bakery in Ludhiana took in ₹1,20,000 last month and made a 20% profit. Her nephew says: "20% of 1,20,000 is 24,000, so she spent ₹96,000." Is he right?</div><div class="chw">Make a quick guess. The answer is in section 20.2.</div></div>
<div class="goals"><h4>By the end of this chapter you will be able to</h4><ul><li>split a total into two parts using a % (girls and boys, passed and failed);</li><li>find expenditure from income and profit %, the correct way;</li><li>find the median of a list;</li><li>read bar charts, line graphs and pie charts;</li><li>solve a five-question DI set in about 8 minutes.</li></ul></div>


<h2><span class="n">20.1</span>Two-part tables: a total and a % of it</h2>
<p>In School P, 800 students passed and 35% of them were girls. The table gives you the total and the girls' share. It does not give the number of girls or boys. You make them.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 116' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><text x='230' y='14' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>School P: 800 students passed</text><rect x='30' y='26' width='140' height='38' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='100' y='50' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Girls 35%</text><rect x='170' y='26' width='260' height='38' rx='0' fill='#17423B' stroke='#fff' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='300' y='50' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#fff' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Boys 100 − 35 = 65%</text><text x='100' y='84' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>35% of 800 = 280</text><text x='300' y='84' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>65% of 800 = 520</text><text x='230' y='108' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>Check: 280 + 520 = 800. The two parts always fill the total.</text></svg></div>
<div class="rule">Girls = total × % girls ÷ 100. Boys = total × (100 − % girls) ÷ 100<small>or simply boys = total − girls</small></div>
<p>Before you answer any question from such a table, build a small table of your own with the parts you need. It takes a minute, and three or four questions can then be read straight from it.</p>
<table class="data"><tr><th>School</th><th>Passed</th><th>% girls</th><th>Girls</th><th>Boys</th></tr><tr><td>P</td><td>800</td><td>35%</td><td>280</td><td>520</td></tr><tr><td>Q</td><td>600</td><td>45%</td><td>270</td><td>330</td></tr></table>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> Q has the higher % of girls (45% against 35%), so many students say Q has more girls. But Q is a smaller school. P has 280 girls and Q has only 270. A % is a share of its own total. When totals differ, compare numbers, never percentages. This is the same lesson as section 19.4.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>School R: 700 students passed and 40% were girls. How many girls and how many boys?</li><li>Find the ratio of girls in Q to boys in R.</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 40% of 700 = 280 girls, 700 − 280 = 420 boys · 2. 270 : 420 = 9 : 14</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">20.2</span>Income, expenditure and profit %</h2>
<p>Joseph runs a tiffin service in Kochi. In March he spent ₹50,000 on vegetables, gas and packing, and he earned ₹60,000. His profit is ₹10,000. As a percentage, profit is always counted on what he spent, just like profit on the cost price in Chapter 5. So his profit is 10,000 ÷ 50,000 = 20%.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 150' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><defs><marker id='ah' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#17423B'/></marker><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 z' fill='#D95D39'/></marker></defs><rect x='70' y='48.0' width='90' height='80.0' rx='4' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='115' y='40.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>₹50,000</text><text x='115' y='144' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Expenditure (cost)</text><rect x='280' y='48.0' width='90' height='80.0' rx='4' fill='#EEF4F2' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><rect x='280' y='32.0' width='90' height='16.0' rx='0' fill='#F2A541' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='1.3'/><text x='325' y='45.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>+10,000</text><text x='325' y='24.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>₹60,000</text><text x='325' y='144' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Income</text><line x1='168' y1='80' x2='272' y2='80' stroke='#17423B' stroke-width='2' marker-end='url(#ah)'/><text x='220' y='72' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>profit 20%</text><text x='220' y='96' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='middle'>of 50,000</text><line x1='40' y1='128' x2='420' y2='128' stroke='#9fb3ae'/></svg></div>
<p>Notice what happened: expenditure is the 100%, and income is 100% + profit %. So income = 120% of expenditure. To go back from income to expenditure, divide by 1.2.</p>
<div class="rule">Income = Expenditure × (100 + profit %) ÷ 100. So Expenditure = Income ÷ (1 + profit % ÷ 100)<small>profit 20% → divide by 1.2 · profit 15% → divide by 1.15 · profit 25% → divide by 1.25</small></div>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> A company earned ₹230 lakh with a profit of 15%. Find its expenditure.<p>230 ÷ 1.15 = <b>₹200 lakh</b>. Check: 15% of 200 is 30, and 200 + 30 = 230.</p></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> do not take the profit % of the income. Income ₹240 lakh with 20% profit: the wrong way is 240 − 20% of 240 = 192. The right way is 240 ÷ 1.2 = 200. Check: 20% of 200 is 40, and 200 + 40 = 240. The check always tells you which one is right.</div>
<div class="rev"><b>⏱ Challenge answer:</b> no. Profit is counted on what she spent, so she spent 1,20,000 ÷ 1.2 = ₹1,00,000. Check: 20% of 1,00,000 is 20,000, and 1,00,000 + 20,000 = 1,20,000.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Income ₹132 lakh, profit 10%. Find the expenditure.</li><li>Income ₹175 lakh, profit 25%. Find the expenditure.</li><li>Expenditure ₹80 lakh, profit 12.5%. Find the income.</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 132 ÷ 1.1 = ₹120 lakh · 2. 175 ÷ 1.25 = ₹140 lakh · 3. 80 × 1.125 = ₹90 lakh</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">20.3</span>The median: the middle value</h2>
<p>Five friends stand in a line from shortest to tallest. The one in the middle is the median height. That is all the median is: the middle value once the list is in order.</p>
<div class="rule">Put the values in order. Odd count: take the middle one. Even count: take the average of the two middle ones.</div>
<div class="ex"><span class="t">Example.</span> Find the median of 140, 120, 170, 150, 130, 160.<p>In order: 120, 130, <b>140, 150</b>, 160, 170. Six values, so take the two in the middle: (140 + 150) ÷ 2 = <b>145</b>.</p></div>
<div class='trap'><b>⚠ Exam trap:</b> the table usually lists values year by year, not in order of size. Picking the middle year without sorting is a common mistake.</div>
<div class='yt'><div class='yth'>✎ Your turn</div><ol><li>Find the median of 7, 3, 9, 4, 8.</li><li>Find the median of 45, 12, 30, 25.</li></ol><div class='yta'>Answers: 1. 3, 4, 7, 8, 9 → 7 · 2. 12, 25, 30, 45 → (25 + 30) ÷ 2 = 27.5</div></div>

<h2><span class="n">20.4</span>Bar charts, line graphs and pie charts</h2>
<p>A chart is a table drawn as a picture. Read each value from it carefully, write the values down as a small table, and then use the same five calculations as before. Here is a bar chart of tea sold at Zoya's stall in Guwahati in one week.</p>
<div class="fig"><svg viewBox='0 0 460 194' width='100%' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><line x1='60' y1='170.0' x2='440' y2='170.0' stroke='#9fb3ae' stroke-width='1'/><text x='52' y='174.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='end'>0</text><line x1='60' y1='132.5' x2='440' y2='132.5' stroke='#EEF4F2' stroke-width='1'/><text x='52' y='136.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='end'>50</text><line x1='60' y1='95.0' x2='440' y2='95.0' stroke='#EEF4F2' stroke-width='1'/><text x='52' y='99.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='end'>100</text><line x1='60' y1='57.5' x2='440' y2='57.5' stroke='#EEF4F2' stroke-width='1'/><text x='52' y='61.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='end'>150</text><line x1='60' y1='20.0' x2='440' y2='20.0' stroke='#EEF4F2' stroke-width='1'/><text x='52' y='24.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#1B2A2F' font-weight='normal' text-anchor='end'>200</text><rect x='85' y='80.0' width='44' height='90.0' rx='2' fill='#17423B'/><text x='107' y='74.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>120</text><text x='107' y='186' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Mon</text><rect x='157' y='57.5' width='44' height='112.5' rx='2' fill='#17423B'/><text x='179' y='51.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>150</text><text x='179' y='186' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Tue</text><rect x='229' y='102.5' width='44' height='67.5' rx='2' fill='#17423B'/><text x='251' y='96.5' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>90</text><text x='251' y='186' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Wed</text><rect x='301' y='35.0' width='44' height='135.0' rx='2' fill='#F2A541'/><text x='323' y='29.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>180</text><text x='323' y='186' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Thu</text><rect x='373' y='50.0' width='44' height='120.0' rx='2' fill='#17423B'/><text x='395' y='44.0' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>160</text><text x='395' y='186' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Fri</text><text x='250' y='14' font-family='Carlito,Calibri,sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#17423B' font-weight='bold' text-anchor='middle'>Cups of tea sold at Zoya's stall</text></svg></div>
<p>A line graph is read in the same way: each point is one value. Read the point against the scale on the left.</p>
<p>A pie chart shows shares of a whole, like the percentage tables of Chapter 19. The full circle is 360°, and it stands for 100%. Here is a family's spending as a pie.</p>
